Ceviri.ekle("en","ekokuma",{
"1806-rusyanin-amaci-bahane-mi":{"baslik": ["What Was Russia's Aim? The Pretext Is Clear; on the Aim the Sources Say “We Do Not Know”", "69ac6e84"], "metin": ["The pretext of a war and its aim are not the same thing, and in this war the gap between the two is unusually wide.\n\nTHE PRETEXT can be measured: the dismissal of the two voivodes without Russian consent and the closing of the Straits to Russian warships — that is, the violation of the alliance of 1805. All the sources describe this in the same way.\n\nTHE AIM cannot be measured. A peer-reviewed article in Belleten openly leaves the question hanging: “The factors that drove the Russians to occupy the Principalities have not yet been satisfactorily explained” (Fehmi İsmail, Belleten XLVI/181, 1982). The same article says that the only direct testimony we have comes from the tsar's own mouth: when the Russian commander-in-chief Kamensky pointed out the drawbacks of opening a second front at a moment when preparations were under way for war with France, Alexander I had attributed the occupation to two reasons — ① to help the Serbs, who were in revolt against the Sublime Porte, ② that since he was not sure how the Ottomans would behave in the future, he would in any case be obliged to keep an army on the Dniester, and therefore preferred occupation to waiting at the frontier.\n\nWas the aim TERRITORY? The article's verdict is cautious: it is reasonable to assume that the Russians did not occupy the Principalities at this time in order to gain territory; indeed, it is clear from the instructions given to Pozzo di Borgo, who was sent in 1807 to make direct contact with the Ottomans, that they did not plan to keep the occupied lands.\n\nBUT at Tilsit the language changed. At Tilsit in July 1807 Napoleon and Alexander discussed the partition of the Ottoman Empire; as a prelude to a general partition, Alexander asked that the Principalities be ceded to Russia, that the eastern Black Sea coast it had occupied from Anapa to Poti be left to it, and that Serbia be established as a principality. The Ottoman side's demands, by contrast, were the exact opposite: the return of all occupied lands, an end to Russian intervention, and Russia's acceptance of the closure of the Straits to all foreign warships.\n\n⇒ the question left to the reader: if the same tsar, within six months, both acted as though saying “I do not want territory” and asked for the Principalities at the partition table — was the occupation of November 1806 a security measure, or a pledge to be turned into territory when the opportunity arose? The source gives no definitive answer to this question; neither does this card. The war would end with the loss of Bessarabia in the Treaty of Bucharest of 1812.", "5a5d624a"]},
"1806-savasin-sebebi-voyvoda-azli":{"sebep.b": ["The dismissal of Constantine Ypsilanti, voivode of Wallachia, and Alexander Mourouzis, voivode of Moldavia, who were considered pro-Russian, before their terms had expired and without obtaining the Russian consent required by the alliance of 1805 (August 1806)", "c7b91ead"], "sonuc.b": ["The Russian army under General Michelson crossing the Dniester without a declaration of war and beginning to occupy Wallachia and Moldavia (23 November 1806; 11 November Julian)", "6789f456"], "bag": ["On paper the pretext is clear: the dismissal of the two voivodes. The real tension, however, was older — the fact that the alliance of 1799 made it impossible for the Ottomans to remain neutral, Russia's right since Küçük Kaynarca to intervene concerning the Principalities and the Orthodox subjects, and the Ottoman rapprochement with France after Austerlitz in order to curtail these rights.", "8628222d"], "metin": ["Background. The Ottoman state had officially declared its neutrality after concluding the Treaty of Paris with France in 1802; but Russia and Britain, maintaining that the alliance made in 1799 was still in force, made this neutrality impossible in practice — in 1805 the Ottomans were even forced under Russian pressure to renew the alliance (Fehmi İsmail, Belleten XLVI/181, 1982). France's defeat of Austria and Russia at the end of 1805 appeared to the Sublime Porte as an opportunity to reduce Russia's privileges and rights of intervention in Ottoman lands. The TDV article `selim-iii` summarizes the same turn as follows: recognizing Napoleon's empire meant returning to a policy weighted toward France and leaving the existing alliance with Britain and Russia, and in 1806 a state of war arose with these two states.\n\nTHE PRETEXT — the dismissal of the two voivodes. Under the privileges granted to Wallachia and Moldavia in 1802, the rulers there were appointed for seven years, and under the alliance of 1805 their replacement was subject to Russian approval. In August 1806 the Sublime Porte dismissed Constantine Ypsilanti, voivode of Wallachia, and Alexander Mourouzis, voivode of Moldavia, whom it considered pro-Russian, before their terms had expired (Pınar Doğanay, ANKARAD 5/10, 2024, pp. 58, 63). Doğanay counts three factors in this decision: the request of the French diplomat who had come to Istanbul, Russia's interference in the internal affairs of Wallachia and Moldavia, and intelligence that the two voivodes were preparing a rebellion in the region. Russia did not recognize the dismissal, insistently demanded the reinstatement of the voivodes and threatened to break off relations otherwise. The Ottomans, who did not want war, agreed to restore the dismissed voivodes to their posts — but this concession did not prevent the war (Fehmi İsmail).\n\nSECOND spark — the Straits. In the summer of 1806 the Ottoman state officially forbade the passage of Russian warships through the Straits; the Russian ambassador Italinsky demanded compliance with the terms of the alliance, but the Sublime Porte did not go back on its decision (Doğanay, pp. 62-63). In response, on 22 September 1806 Britain demanded that French influence be ended, that Russian ships be allowed through the Strait and that the voivodes be reinstated.\n\nTHE OCCUPATION. According to Doğanay, on 16 October 1806 Russia ordered its armies to move; a Russian army of 60,000 crossed the Dniester (Turla) and occupied Wallachia and Moldavia. This page's own record gives the start of the crossing as 11 November Julian = 23 November 1806; Fehmi İsmail likewise says that “in November 1806 the Russian occupation forces entered the Principalities”. The two sources do not contradict each other; they date two different things: 16 October is the date of the order to move, and November that of the actual crossing. Doğanay's statement in the same sentence that “on 16 October 1806 it had declared war on the Ottoman state” is inconsistent within the article itself — two lines later she writes that Russia acted without declaring war; in this card it has been taken not as a declaration but as the order to move. The commanders of the fortresses of Khotyn, Bender, Akkerman and Kilia surrendered their fortresses without a fight; only the commandant of Izmail resisted.\n\nTHE DECLARATION of war. The Ottoman state took up the question of war at the meeting of the Imperial Council (Divan-ı Hümayun) of 20 December 1806 and decided to declare war on Russia; the proposal of neutrality made by some peace-minded statesmen did not prevail against popular agitation and the pressure of the French ambassador (Doğanay, p. 64). The atlas chronology places the beginning of the war on 22 December 1806.", "38f80f4e"]},
"ahi-birlikleri-ankara":{"sebep.b": ["The collapse of the central authority of the Anatolian Seljuks with the rout at Köse Dağ, and the transformation of the religious futuwwa brotherhoods of tradesmen (the Akhis) into de facto local governments in many cities", "f5af3ed5"], "sonuc.b": ["The incorporation of Akhi-governed Ankara into Ottoman rule by Süleyman Pasha, son of Orhan Gazi", "cdc574b4"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on Akhism (Ahîlik), these brotherhoods became the local authority in many Anatolian cities, especially during the Mongol invasion; Ankara is the best-documented example (Köprülü, “Ankara ve Ahîler” [Ankara and the Akhis], 1926), and the city remained under this rule until 1354. The sources differ on the day of Köse Dağ (Ibn Bibi says 1 July 1243, Ibn al-Adim 3 July 1243).", "565fc5b3"], "metin": ["Akhism was an organization of tradesmen and craftsmen born of the futuwwa (chivalry and generosity) tradition of the Islamic world; it was organized in the grades of sheikh, khalifa and naqib, and regulated entry into a trade through the sequence of apprentice, journeyman and master, as well as the control of goods and prices. When central authority weakened, it could cease to be merely a guild association and take over the de facto government of a city — this is exactly how Ankara was governed until 1354; that is why the map shows Ankara as held by the 'Akhis' during that window.", "f068337e"]},
"alemdar-28-temmuz-1808-kargasa":{"sebep.b": ["Alemdar Mustafa Pasha's storming of the Sublime Porte and his march on Topkapı Palace on the morning of 28 July 1808", "8c6dd5a5"], "sonuc.b": ["The murder of Selim III, the rescue of Prince Mahmud by spiriting him away over the roof, and the accession of Mahmud II", "6454a1a6"], "bag": ["The coup was carried out to put Selim III back on the throne; its result was Selim's death and the accession of the only surviving male member of the dynasty — the exact opposite of what had been intended. The subject of this card is how this contrary result came about: the sequence of the hours.", "606a47a9"], "surec": ["The start of the chain, a year earlier. According to the TDV article `kabakci-isyani`, the Nizam-i Cedid had begun to be shaken from 1805, and the attempt to apply it in Rumelia had failed “as a result of the general resistance of the ayan and local strongmen and of armed clashes” (the Second Edirne Incident, 1806). After the army left Istanbul on 12 April 1807, “the intention of bringing” the yamaks (auxiliary garrison troops) in the Bosphorus forts “into the Nizam-i Cedid system” opened the door to an agitation in which the same article regards the deputy grand vizier Köse Musa Pasha and the Shaykh al-Islam Topal Ataullah Efendi as “the real architects behind the scenes of the uprising”; on 25 May 1807 Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki, commander of the Hungarian battery, were killed, and the revolt began. Although he had more than 10,000 drilled soldiers at his disposal, Selim III “sacrificed the officials demanded” and abolished the Nizam-i Cedid with all its institutions; even so, he could not remain on the throne: Mustafa IV became sultan.\n\nTHE PREPARATION of the countercoup. The statesmen who supported the Nizam-i Cedid took refuge with Alemdar Mustafa Pasha on the Danube front, who was ayan of Rusçuk, governor of Silistra and commander-in-chief (serasker) of the Danube. According to the TDV article `alemdar-mustafa-pasa`, the plan of Abdullah Ramiz, Mehmed Tahsin, Mustafa Refik, Mehmed Said Galib and Mehmed Emin Behiç Efendis, “who have gone down in history under the name of the 'Rusçuk friends' (Rusçuk yaranı)”, was this: by currying favor with the new sultan, the Nizam-i Cedid would be revived and Selim III placed back on the throne. When it was heard that the close circle of Mustafa IV was preparing to kill Selim, they made haste; the army marched from Edirne to Istanbul.\n\nTWO preliminary moves. ① While Alemdar was still on the way, Kabakçı Mustafa, who had obtained the post of Superintendent of the Bosphorus after the revolt, was killed: according to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, this was done by “Ali Agha, the ayan of Pınarhisar”, sent by Alemdar; the article `kabakci-isyani` says that this was “fifteen days before Mahmud II was placed on the throne”. ② On 19 July 1808 the army and the Sacred Standard (sancak-ı şerif) were received on the plain of Davud Pasha by Mustafa IV in person; Alemdar rejected Ramiz Efendi's proposal “let us arrest the sultan at once”, saying that such a move “would be unseemly” at the very moment when the Sacred Standard was being received. Nine days passed in restoring order in the city and punishing those who had deposed Selim.", "f61f490a"], "metin": ["28 July 1808, the early hours — and why so early. TDV `alemdar-mustafa-pasa`: “On 28 July Alemdar set out early with the forces at his side, seized the important points of the city and marched on Topkapı Palace. The reason for this sudden and early move was that his intention of putting Selim III back on the throne had become known.” The Sublime Porte was stormed and the seal was taken from the grand vizier by force. Through the Shaykh al-Islam Arapzade Arif Efendi, Alemdar “sent word” to Mustafa IV “that he should withdraw from the throne and that Selim III should be placed on it”, and he charged the chief black eunuch (kızlar ağası) with getting Selim ready. In other words, the coup had been designed as an abdication without bloodshed.\n\nTHE PALACE'S answer: the gates and two death orders. The same article: “In the face of these developments, Mustafa IV ordered the palace gates to be shut and permitted the killing of his uncle Selim III and his brother Prince Mahmud.” The article `mustafa-iv` describes the same moment from Mustafa IV's side: “Mustafa shut the palace gates and ordered the killing of his cousin Selim and his brother Mahmud.” The difference between the two articles is not trivial: one says “permitted”, the other “ordered” — this difference of tone between two articles by the same author (Kemal Beydilli) is a tension within the source itself, and this card does not resolve it.\n\nTHE KILLING of Selim III. The TDV article `selim-iii` is the place that gives the scene in the greatest detail: “The coup began with the assault on the palace on 4 Jumada al-Akhira 1223 (28 July 1808) and reached the point of life and death in the afternoon.” Selim “attempted to defend himself and had to grapple with some twenty killers, headed by the aghas Başçuhadar Abdülfettah, Kethüda Ebe Selim and Nezir, the deputy of the treasury, all of whom would later be caught and executed.” The manner of death comes in two accounts: “it has been related that when Ebe Selim squeezed his testicles, the executioner threw the cord around him and strangled him”, whereas the marks of blows on his body and the condition in which “the skin of his right temple had been torn down to his chin together with his beard” (Cevdet, VIII, 308) “indicate that he was martyred in a bloody manner.” When Alemdar broke down the gates and entered, he came upon “Selim's body, laid on a mattress on the platform in front of the door of the Audience Chamber (Arz Odası) facing the Gate of Felicity (Bâbüssaâde)”.\n\nHOW Prince Mahmud was rescued. The TDV articles on Selim and Alemdar pass over this part in a single sentence (“At this time only Prince Mahmud could be saved”; “Mahmud had been saved by escaping with difficulty”). The details are in another article of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, Semavi Eyice's article `cevri-kalfa-mektebi`: “In the summer of 1223 (1808), while Prince Mahmud was being spirited away through the efforts of a few of his supporters from the hands of those who had come to kill him, Cevri Kalfa too helped the prince to escape by throwing ashes into the eyes of the executioners who were trying to climb the stairs.” The long version of the scene is reported in the academic literature as follows (Ceylan 2019, p. 333; citing Cunbur 1985, p. 77): when the killers advanced on the Harem, the chief tutor Tayyar Agha escaped with difficulty and took the news to Receb Paşazade Mehmed Bey, the prince's former tutor, in the Seferli Dormitory; three aghas (Anber, Kasım, Hafız İsa) who ran to the Kuşhane gate found first Selim's and then Mahmud's apartment empty — because “Cevri Kalfa, on hearing the noise, had already intervened in the matter earlier and had spirited the Prince away to her own apartment by way of the Golden Road (Altın Yol)”. At the head of the stone staircase “three men against nineteen swords” held out; when Kasım Agha fell, wounded by a spear, Cevri Kalfa came out with a bowl of ashes in her hand, and while telling the aghas to take the prince away over the roof, she flung “handfuls of ashes into the killers' faces”. When her ashes ran out, she was kicked in the stomach and knocked unconscious; those who entered the room saw that the prince “was being hoisted on shoulders through the skylight onto the roof”, and a dagger hurled by Ebe Selim wounded Mahmud in the wrist. This long narrative is secondhand: Cunbur 1985 and Cevdet Pasha's text were not opened; the core confirmed by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is the delaying with ashes and the escape over the roof.\n\nTHE SOUND of cannon and the first order. The article `selim-iii`: “At around 4 o'clock cannon fire began to be heard from Sarayburnu; this was the sign of the change of reign, but like the other inhabitants of Istanbul, the embassies in Pera did not yet know who had come to the throne. It was generally supposed that Selim had regained his throne.” An hour later the criers proclaimed that the new sultan was Mahmud II. The TDV article `mahmud-ii--osmanli` treats the new sultan's condition at that moment as evidence of his character: “as can also be understood from his conduct when, having only just returned from the brink of death, he gave his first orders to Alemdar Mustafa Pasha, he is extremely cool-headed.”\n\nTHE AFTERMATH: Four months. Alemdar became grand vizier and had Selim's killers arrested one by one; Köse Musa Pasha and Tayyar Pasha were executed. Mustafa IV was confined to his apartment in the palace, and after Alemdar was killed, as the article `mustafa-iv` records, he was strangled on 17 November 1808 on the order of his own brother. The measured warning in the same article is also the limit of this card: because Mustafa IV was deposed “without being given the opportunity to take any action that would express his real thoughts about innovations”, the definitive judgments about him are “in need of correction”.", "b80d5f7f"]},
"alemdar-mustafa-pasa-sahsiyet":{"baslik": ["What Kind of Man Was Alemdar Mustafa Pasha? From a Steward Engaged in Stock Raising to Grand Vizier", "a3d4d5be"], "metin": ["Biographical record. TDV `alemdar-mustafa-pasa` (Kemal Beydilli): 1765-1808, “ayan of Rusçuk, grand vizier.” He was born in Khotyn; his father was “Hacı Hasan Agha, one of the Janissaries of Rusçuk”. He first entered the Janissary Corps, then “began to engage in stock raising and farming in Rusçuk” and entered the service of Tirsinikli İsmail Agha, the most powerful ayan (provincial notable) of the region: “Thanks to his ability he rose in a short time. As his steward (kethüda), he became his principal assistant in all his activities.” His nickname also comes from this — according to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, “from his having served as Tirsinikli's standard-bearer” (alemdar).\n\nTHE STEPS of his rise are concrete. On account of his successes in the struggle against the rebel Pazvandoğlu, he received the rank of kapıcıbaşı (chief gatekeeper), then the office of ayan of Hezargrad; Tirsinikli's sudden death (12 August 1806) was the turning point. Alemdar, who came quickly to Rusçuk and took control of the situation, “was chosen 'ayan of ayans' by the agreement of the ayan in the lands under Tirsinikli's control and all the other ayan subject to him.” The central government did not welcome this; “although at first it recognized him only as ayan of Rusçuk, after a while it was obliged to recognize his dominion over a region far larger than that under Tirsinikli's control.” An archive-based academic study shows with figures why this recognition was unavoidable: the Sublime Porte tried to prevent Alemdar from becoming dominant in İsmail Agha's sphere of influence, but “was unable to prevent the domination in the region of Alemdar, who had won the support of the people and of the ayan of 41 kazas (districts) subject to İsmail Agha” (Bayrak 2009, p. 64, note 7; BOA hat 43/2189, 63/2771). On 4 February 1807 Selim III gave him, with the rank of vizier, the governorship of Silistra for life and the post of commander-in-chief of the Danube; in the war he saved the fortress of Izmail, crossed the Danube and routed the Russian general Michelson.\n\nTHE MATERIAL basis of his power. Alemdar's political weight was not only military: the probate registers (muhallefat/tereke) drawn up after his death paint the portrait of a tax farmer-ayan who held tax units such as the customs of Rusçuk and the zecriye (excise on liquor) of Tarnovo, Hezargrad and Sistova in his tax farm, who made expenditures in advance on behalf of the districts, and who is thought to have extended credit to ayan and artisans; his claims have been calculated at 1,391,741 kuruş and his debts at 908,048 kuruş (Bayrak 2009, pp. 96-97). According to the article, the state decided on “the confiscation of all of Alemdar's property, which constituted the source of his political power” — that is, his contemporaries too saw the source of his power in his wealth. The total he received as the income of office during his grand vizierate (28 July-16 November 1808) was 182,978.5 kuruş (ibid., p. 78).\n\nCHARACTER — in the source's own sentence. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's verdict carries praise and criticism in the same sentence: “Alemdar, who was forty-three years old when he died, was brave and patriotic; although uneducated, he was extremely intelligent and quick to grasp things, a personality who, when well guided, could get things done and be useful to the state.” The remark that follows immediately is also an interpretation of character: “the atmosphere of Istanbul, in which he lived for a few months, had produced a surprising change in his character and plunged him into great heedlessness.” This “heedlessness” is not abstract: the same article counts as the direct cause of his death the fact that Alemdar and his friends “abandoned every kind of precaution and underestimated the threats that might come from the Janissaries and other opponents”. Two concrete records concerning how far he could be guided: ① his rejection of Ramiz Efendi's proposal to arrest the sultan (19 July 1808) shows that he used his own judgment; ② his appointment as grand vizier came about “especially on the advice of the 'friends' and through his own personal application to the new sultan”. As the rise of an ayan to the grand vizierate in Ottoman history, the academic literature counts him “among the interesting figures of Ottoman history… who lived in the period dominated by the ayan, who took the place of central authority in the provinces, and who left his mark on this period” (Bayrak 2009, p. 63).\n\nTHE BALANCE sheet of four months. TDV: “The most important of the things Alemdar did during his four-month grand vizierate was to invite the ayan of Anatolia and Rumelia to Istanbul and conclude a compact that has gone down in our histories under the name of the Sened-i İttifak (Deed of Alliance) (October 1808).” The second is the Sekban-i Cedid: “a new military corps in the nature of a continuation of the Nizam-i Cedid troops”, whose command was given to Kadı Abdurrahman Pasha. The fate of his body also tells of the political memory of the period: “Alemdar's bones, which had been thrown by the ruffians into a dry well outside Yedikule, were removed from there after the abolition of the Janissary Corps and buried near the walls of Yedikule, and after the proclamation of the Second Constitutional Era they were transferred to the graveyard of the Zeyneb Sultan Mosque.”", "6f31a310"]},
"alemdar-vakasi-mahmudun-tavri":{"baslik": ["The Alemdar Incident: could Mahmud II have saved him, and did he want to?", "0cf9735a"], "ozet": ["When the Sublime Porte was besieged in November 1808, the grand vizier was the man who had put the sultan on the throne four months earlier. No help came. The sources diverge on the reason: was there no force, was there no will, or did the outcome suit the new sultan? All three readings emerge from the same texts — the card takes no side and sets out all three with their supporting evidence.", "d61c2d9c"], "metin": ["The event. The TDV article `alemdar-mustafa-pasa` summarises the incident as follows: Alemdar and his associates were caught unprepared because they \"showed great heedlessness, abandoned every kind of precaution and underestimated the threats that might come from the Janissaries and other opponents\"; \"as a result of the uprising that began on the night of 15 November 1808, Alemdar was attacked at the Sublime Porte and his residence was surrounded.\" He continued to resist, and \"when the help he was expecting did not arrive in time, at the moment when the Janissaries were about to break in, he set fire to the powder magazine and died himself together with hundreds of Janissary thugs (16 November 1808).\" The uprising did not end there: among his associates, Refik, Tahsin and Galib Efendis were killed, and \"even the sultan himself and the palace were put under siege.\" The soldiers of the new Sekban corps (Sekbân-ı Cedîd) fought the Janissaries street by street at Tophane, Galata, Üsküdar and around the palace; some 4,000 untrained sekbans could not hold out against 15,000-16,000 Janissaries, and when their commander Süleyman Agha also died, they scattered (TDV `sekban-i-cedid`).\n\nThe FIRST reading — there was no force. This is the explanation given by the TDV article `alemdar-mustafa-pasa` itself: \"The palace, which did not have sufficient force at hand to use against the rebels, was put in a state of defence by Râmiz and Kadı Abdurrahman Pashas after the bewilderment and hesitation had passed, and for this reason help to Alemdar was delayed.\" In other words, the small number of trained troops available to the palace was used first to protect the sultan; the failure to reach Alemdar in time was the result not of a choice but of a priority.\n\nThe SECOND reading — there was already a distance. The TDV article describing the founding of the new Sekban corps states plainly that the sultan kept his distance from the new army from the outset: \"Mahmud II, who evidently viewed with unease the undertaking of so dangerous a task as forming a trained army at the very beginning of his reign, reacted against this development, but was obliged to consent when Alemdar Mustafa Pasha stated that he had obtained sealed pledges from the officers of the infantry and cavalry corps of the standing army (kapıkulu).\" The same article counts the sultan himself among those waiting to see the outcome: \"Within their own political calculations, those who feared that events might end, as before, in a Janissary revolt, Mahmud II foremost among them, behaved with reserve toward the new formation.\" In other words, the grand vizier's project was proceeding not with the sultan's consent but without it.\n\nThe THIRD reading — the outcome worked in his favour. The TDV article `ayan` (by Özcan Mert) records the outcome directly as a gain: \"In this uprising, which broke out on 16 November 1808, Alemdar Mustafa Pasha lost his life. Thus Mahmud II not only got rid of the most powerful ayan close to him, but the domination of the ayan in Istanbul also came to an end. In addition, the Deed of Alliance became void.\" To this is added the advice given by his chief valet (başçuhadar) Eğriburun Ömer Agha when the sultan was ratifying the deed, advice which the sultan followed: \"that the deed be ratified and later done away with\" (TDV `sened-i-ittifak`). According to this reading, the sultan's attitude had begun not in November but in October.\n\nA second decision on the SAME night. There is a fact that tests the claim of indecision: while the uprising was besieging the palace, the rebels had a legitimate alternative at hand — Mustafa IV, confined in the princes' quarters (the kafes, or \"cage\"). The TDV article `mustafa-iv`: \"When Alemdar was attacked and killed some three months later and the rebels sought to march on the palace, Mustafa was, on his brother's orders, strangled with the sash from his own waist by Abdullah Râmiz, Kadı Abdurrahman Pasha, Seyyid Ali Efendi and İnce Mehmed Bey, and so killed (28 Ramadan 1223 / 17 November 1808).\" In other words, when his own throne was at stake, the young sultan did not hesitate and eliminated the dynasty's other male member. This does not refute the \"he had no force\" reading (having one man strangled is not enough to rescue an army), but it does put strain on the thesis that \"he had no will\".\n\nWhere the SULTAN'S personality stands in this debate. The TDV article `mahmud-ii--osmanli` describes him as \"capable, resolute and hard-working\" and \"extremely cool-headed, as can also be understood from his conduct at the moment when, having only just returned from the brink of death, he gave Alemdar Mustafa Pasha his first orders\", and it records one of his habits: \"That from the very first day he kept a list of people of this kind, to be eliminated when the opportunity later arose, and that he brought it out and used it when the time came.\" The same article regards Alemdar's grand vizierate and the Deed of Alliance as part of a period passed \"under the domination\" of the ayan camp. Waiting and striking when the time had come is also the method he was to use eighteen years later when abolishing the Janissary corps — TDV regards that decision as \"an indication of how well Mahmud II had followed developments and how accurately he had chosen the most suitable moment\". Whether 1808 was the first example of this method or merely a moment of helplessness is not settled in the sources consulted.", "404abfc0"], "bag": ["View: The sultan used the only regular force he had to protect his own life; help was delayed, not withheld (TDV `alemdar-mustafa-pasa`). Counter-view: From the very beginning the sultan viewed this army \"with unease\" and held himself \"with reserve\", and as a result of the event he \"got rid of the most powerful ayan close to him\", while the Deed of Alliance also became void (TDV `sekban-i-cedid`, `ayan`, `sened-i-ittifak`). A third possibility — intention and outcome are separate things: the same event may both have been experienced in real helplessness and have served the sultan's interest; none of the sources provides any record that the sultan deliberately blocked help.", "18165a55"]},
"antlasma-berlin-1878":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Berlin (1878) — Greater Bulgaria Split into Three", "0741a241"], "metin": ["Berlin divided into three the Greater Bulgaria created by San Stefano. ① A Principality of Bulgaria with reduced borders: an entity tributary to the Ottoman state and free in its internal affairs, whose prince was to be elected by the people and appointed upon confirmation by the Sublime Porte and with the consent of the Great Powers, and in which there would be no Ottoman troops. ② The province of Eastern Rumelia: administratively independent but politically and militarily subject to the Ottomans; it was to be administered by a Christian governor appointed for a term of five years by the Sublime Porte with the approval of the European states. ③ Macedonia: left to the Ottomans on condition that reforms be carried out. In addition, the independence of Serbia was recognized and Niš and Pirot were given to it; some territory was to be given to Greece; Bosnia and Herzegovina was to be occupied by Austria. On the eastern front, Kars, Ardahan and Batum were ceded to Russia in lieu of part of the war indemnity, while Doğubayazıt and the Eleşkirt valley remained with the Ottomans. The amount of the indemnity was determined later: by the Treaty of Istanbul of 8 February 1879, after deducting the value of the places ceded to Russia, it was fixed at 802,500,000 francs and it was decided that it would be paid in twenty-one equal installments over seven years; on 14 May 1882 it was converted into annual installments of 350,000 liras, with the tithe (âşâr) tax pledged as security.", "569e057b"]},
"antlasma-karlofca-1699":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Karlowitz (1699) — Provisions Front by Front", "16ac3200"], "metin": ["Hungarian front: All of Hungary, including Transylvania but excluding the province of Temeşvar (the Banat), was ceded to Austria. The frontier was accepted as a straight line from the point where the Tisza flows into the Danube to the point where the waters of the Bosut join the Sava; the Bačka side remained with Austria, and the Titel district was left as before. Austria asked that Imre Thököly, who had fought on the Ottoman side, and his followers withdraw from the Hungarian borders; Rami Mehmed Efendi accepted this on condition that those of his supporters and relatives remaining in Hungary be able to pass freely into Ottoman lands. Russian front: Azov and its surroundings were ceded to Russia; in response, the Ottoman government had Yeni Kale built on the Kerch Strait for the security of the Black Sea. One demand, however, was rejected: the Russian envoy's request for free navigation of merchant ships on the Black Sea was not accepted — the same right would be granted seventy-five years later at Küçük Kaynarca.", "71c125f1"]},
"antlasma-kucuk-kaynarca-1774":{"baslik": ["Küçük Kaynarca (1774) — The Crimea, Fortresses and Indemnity", "c498cc78"], "metin": ["There were three issues on which the negotiations became deadlocked: granting autonomy to the Crimea (which was considered contrary to the sharia), the surrender of fortresses regarded as militarily vital, and the indemnity. The provisions agreed were as follows: the independence of the Crimea was recognized. Kerch, Yeni Kale and Kinburn remained in Russian hands; while Ochakov (Özi) was left to Russia, Bender, opposite it, was handed over to the Ottomans. All the places Russia had captured during the war, including the Principalities (Wallachia and Moldavia), Bessarabia, and the fortresses of Akkerman, Kilia, Izmail and Bender, were to be returned; the places it had captured in the Mediterranean were also to be returned (art. 7). Permission was to be given for the construction in Beyoğlu, on the main road, of an Orthodox church of the Russian rite, and Russia was to assume the protection of this church. Russian pilgrims were to be able to visit the holy places freely. Russian merchant ships were to navigate freely on the Black Sea and the Mediterranean, benefit from the privileges granted to other states, and be able to open consulates wherever necessary — the demand that had been rejected at Karlowitz. The Ottoman state was to pay an indemnity of 15,000 purses (4.5 million rubles) in three installments (additional separate article).", "35e6e26b"]},
"antlasma-pasarofca-1718":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Passarowitz (1718) — Austrian and Venetian Provisions", "308ece1f"], "metin": ["Two separate treaties were signed. With Austria, twenty articles: the first seven concerned the determination and fixing of the frontiers, and the final demarcation was left to commissioners. The whole of the Banat, the western half of Wallachia up to the Olt River (Little Wallachia), the northern part of Serbia including Belgrade, and northern Bosnia were ceded to Austria. In the subsequent articles the terms of Karlowitz were generally followed: ensuring security in the event of mutual encroachments, protection of the Catholic clergy of the Holy Sepulchre in Jerusalem, and the release or exchange of prisoners. With Venice, twenty-six articles: Venice demanded all the places it had lost in the last war, together with two years' indemnity; the Ottomans returned only the island of Cerigo (Çuka), which they had seized during the war — on condition that a free road be left to Venice between Bosnia and Ragusa and that the three palisaded forts on this route be brought within the Ottoman frontier. The Morea remained with the Ottomans. Trade: in response to the demand for indemnity, the customs duty on imports and exports was reduced from 5% to 3%; the first four articles of the commercial treaty allowed Austrian merchants to trade freely in Ottoman lands with a one-time customs duty of 3%. All types of coin on the market were to be accepted, and no customs duty was to be levied on money and precious metals.", "190350ef"]},
"antlasma10-albuquerque-kocin-1503":{"baslik": ["Albuquerque's expedition to Cochin (1503) — the Raja of Cochin came under Portuguese protection, and the first Portuguese fort in Asia was built", "7ddb7048"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. Rather than a written treaty, this chronology entry marks the establishment of the relationship of protection and alliance between the Raja of Cochin and the Portuguese. According to Britannica, Afonso de Albuquerque came to Cochin in 1503 with his cousin Francisco, undertook to protect the raja, had the first Portuguese fort in Asia built and placed a garrison in it. He then established a trading post at Kollam (Quilon) and returned to Lisbon in July 1504. Britannica's article on Cochin likewise writes that the first European fort in Kochi was built by Albuquerque in 1503 and that the city remained in Portuguese hands until the Dutch took it in 1663.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. Cochin was a port kingdom subject to the Zamorin of Calicut. When the Zamorin became hostile to the Portuguese under the influence of the Muslim merchants who controlled the spice trade, his vassal Cochin welcomed the Portuguese. Portugal thus gained its first permanent fortification and a local ally on the Malabar coast of India; Goa was not to be conquered and made Portugal's naval base until 1510.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. After Vasco da Gama reached India in 1498, Cabral established the first European settlement at Cochin in 1500, and da Gama opened a trading station there in 1502. Albuquerque's fort deepened the conflict with the Zamorin: Duarte Pacheco Pereira, who came to India in 1503, defended the Portuguese trading post at Cochin the following year against the attacks of the ruler of Calicut. With the dispatch of Francisco de Almeida as the first viceroy in 1505, these coastal bases turned into a systematic Portuguese maritime empire.", "a57f9e6a"]},
"antlasma10-alcanices-1297":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Alcañices (1297) — Castile left Riba-Côa and places around the Guadiana to Portugal; the foundation of one of Europe's oldest borders was laid", "ffc4a05c"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. On 12 September 1297 at Alcañices (Alcanizes in Portuguese sources), King Dinis of Portugal and King Ferdinand IV of Castile, in the presence of the nobles and clergy of both sides, signed an agreement defining the border between the two kingdoms. By the agreement Olivença, Campo Maior, Ouguela and San Felices de los Gallegos, together with the places of Riba-Côa beyond the river Côa, were left to Portugal; the places on the left bank of the Guadiana were also set aside to be given to Portugal. For Aroche and Aracena, which remained with Castile, the rights of the bishopric of Badajoz were mentioned separately. According to Britannica, the agreement also confirmed that the Algarve belonged to Portugal and established an alliance between the two kingdoms. A marriage arrangement between the heirs of the two thrones was also added to the provisions.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. Historiography regards this agreement as the beginning of one of Europe's oldest border lines. The line drawn was not entirely new, but it brought under Portuguese control many places that had frequently been disputed. Although its implementation was delayed in some places, most of the places given to Portugal sooner or later became part of the Portuguese kingdom. Britannica notes that Dinis succeeded in establishing a definitive border with Castile.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. The agreement was the product of a balance in Portugal's favour. At the end of the 13th century, while Castile was shaken by turmoil over the throne, Dinis's rule was at its height. In the Treaty of Badajoz of 1267, by contrast, Afonso III had left to Castile all the places between the Guadiana and the Guadalquivir (including Aroche and Aracena). Alcañices made permanent the gains that had been promised to Dinis once again in 1295. Some historians read it as a peace treaty rather than a document drawing a border.", "f7b70b6d"]},
"antlasma10-arras-1482":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Arras (1482) — the Burgundian inheritance was divided between France and the Habsburgs; Artois and Franche-Comté passed to France as Margaret's dowry", "18e94688"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. The treaty signed at Arras between King Louis XI of France and Archduke Maximilian of Austria divided between the two sides the Burgundian inheritance left by Charles the Bold. Louis retained full sovereignty over the Duchy of Burgundy, Picardy and the Boulonnais. Margaret (Margaret of Austria), the young daughter of Maximilian and Mary of Burgundy, was betrothed to the dauphin, Louis's son and heir, the future Charles VIII; Franche-Comté and Artois were handed over to France as the dowry of this betrothal. The rest of the Burgundian inheritance, above all Flanders and the Low Countries, remained with Mary's son Philip.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. Britannica regards Louis XI's acquisition of Franche-Comté and Artois in 1482 as the final link in his policy of enlarging the territory of the kingdom. The treaty marks the beginning of the definitive division of the Burgundian state into French and Habsburg shares: while the duchy, considered a French fief, remained with France, the Low Countries passed into the hands of the House of Habsburg. This division laid the ground for the quarrel between France and the Habsburgs over the north-eastern border during the following two hundred years.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. When Charles the Bold died at Nancy on 5 January 1477, Louis set about breaking up the Burgundian state and annexing its French fiefs to the kingdom. The heiress Mary, despite French pressure, refused to marry the dauphin and in 1477 married Maximilian; Maximilian defended the inheritance. With Mary's death in 1482 Maximilian was weakened: the States General of the Low Countries took over the regency on behalf of the young Philip, and Maximilian was forced to consent to his daughter's betrothal to the French heir. However, when Charles VIII broke off the betrothal and sent Margaret back to her father, Franche-Comté and Artois were returned to the Habsburgs by the Treaty of Senlis of 1493.", "f2473e78"]},
"antlasma10-basel-1499":{"baslik": ["Peace of Basel (1499) — the Swabian (Swiss) War ended; the Thurgau high court passed to the Swiss Confederation", "3c4b77b0"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. After preliminary talks at Schaffhausen and the armistice of 25 August 1499, peace was signed at Basel on 22 September 1499 through the mediation of Ludovico Sforza, Duke of Milan, and his envoy Galeazzo Visconti. On one side stood King Maximilian I and the Swabian League, on the other the Swiss Confederation, Bishop Heinrich of Chur and the Three Leagues (Graubünden). At first sight the treaty restored the situation that had existed before the war; only two clauses touched on rights of sovereignty: Maximilian kept the eight jurisdictions in the Prättigau, while the Thurgau high court (Landgericht), which belonged to the city of Constance, was subsequently given to the Confederation in October 1499. Maximilian was to lift all hostile measures, bans and lawsuits, and in future disputes was to accept as arbitrators the bishops of Constance or Basel, or the city council of Basel.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. The real causes of the war — the Common Penny tax decided at the Diet of Worms in 1495 and the Imperial Chamber Court (Reichskammergericht) — were not mentioned at all in the text; the arbitration clause sidelined this court without saying so explicitly. Britannica writes that the Confederation did not comply with the decisions of Worms and, although the empire had very little influence left over it, remained attached to the empire, and that the peace was an important step on the road to its de facto independence. Older Swiss historiography had claimed that independence was recognised here; the Historical Dictionary of Switzerland (HLS) states that this view is today considered refuted and that the Swiss cantons continued to belong to the empire until the 17th century.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. The war began when Maximilian, together with the Swabian League, attacked Graubünden, an ally of the Confederation; there was fighting in Graubünden and along the Rhine from Basel to Vorarlberg. Having lost the war, Maximilian was obliged to agree to peace in the same year; at about the same time the French returned to Italy and occupied Milan, an imperial fief.", "7df422c1"]},
"antlasma10-deulino-1618":{"baslik": ["Truce of Deulino (1618) — Smolensk, Chernigov and Novgorod-Seversk remained with Poland for 14.5 years", "913f5d18"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. The truce signed on 11 December 1618 between the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and the Tsardom of Muscovy halted hostilities for fourteen and a half years. Smolensk and the other western Russian lands seized by Poland remained on the Polish side; according to the Encyclopedia of Ukraine, the Chernigov region, together with Starodub and Novgorod-Seversk, also passed to Poland. The truce ended the campaign that the Polish prince Władysław had been waging to take the throne of Moscow.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. The Encyclopedia of Ukraine regards Deulino and the campaigns that followed it as the high point of Polish eastward expansion; thereafter the Commonwealth moved toward decline, and Muscovy toward becoming the greatest power in Eastern Europe. In 1635 Poland created the Chernigov Voivodeship out of the northern part of the Chernigov region (Chernigov, Novgorod-Seversk, Konotop, Putivl, Glinsk) and attached the southern part (Nizhyn, Oster, Baturyn) to the Kiev Voivodeship. The harsh exploitation under Polish administration and the seizure by Polish nobles of the lands of local peasants and townspeople drove the region into the Cossack uprisings of 1637-1638.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. During the Time of Troubles Poland intervened in Muscovy; Smolensk was besieged in 1609, and in August 1610 the boyars of Moscow accepted Władysław as tsar. When Michael Romanov came to the throne in 1613, Władysław maintained his claim to it, and war broke out again in 1617. Inconclusive fighting led to peace negotiations, and the negotiations to Deulino. When the term expired in 1632, war broke out once more; the Russians were unable to retake Smolensk, and the Treaty of Polyanovka of 1634 essentially confirmed the settlement of 1618.", "7a46aac4"]},
"antlasma10-feragat-bildirgesi-1581":{"baslik": ["Act of Abjuration (Plakkaat van Verlatinghe, 1581) — the States General declared Philip II's rule forfeit", "df90d54b"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. On 26 July 1581 the States General (Staten-Generaal) of the rebellious Netherlandish provinces declared that King Philip II of Spain had lost his sovereignty over the provinces through his persistent oppression. The document is a 'plakkaat', that is, a decree with its reasons stated: it sets out the principles that a ruler is placed by God to protect his subjects and that subjects are not created for the sake of the ruler; that a ruler who oppresses his subjects, crushes them with heavy taxes and tramples on their ancient rights and privileges is to be considered a tyrant, and that the people, together with the States, may abandon him and choose another protector in his place. It then lists grievances such as religious persecution and the Tenth Penny tax. In the operative part, Philip's sovereignty over these countries is revoked; the States provide for the use of their own seals, the minting of coin and the taking of an oath of allegiance to the States. The text was drafted by Jan van Asseliers, secretary of the States.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. Britannica describes the act as a declaration of independence for the whole of the Netherlands. The historian Anton van Hooff, however, points out that the document did not yet aim at full independence: the text assumed that François, Duke of Anjou, would be the new ruler of at least some of the provinces. Even so, the sovereign dispositions concerning the seal, coinage and oath, and the emphasis on 'inborn freedom', make the document an early declaration of rights.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. The Union of Utrecht, formed on 23 January 1579 by Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, Gelderland and Zutphen, had bound the northern provinces more closely together in order to continue the resistance to Spain; the act is the legal break of this process. After the disappointments experienced with Anjou and Leicester, on 12 April 1588 the States General made the Council of State the highest organ of government; thus the search for a king was abandoned and the Dutch Republic was born.", "b82f836e"]},
"antlasma10-fribourg-ebedi-baris-1516":{"baslik": ["Perpetual Peace of Fribourg (1516) — after Marignano, a peace of unlimited duration between the Swiss Confederation and France; Ticino remained Swiss", "c2a3feb4"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. On 29 November 1516 at Fribourg (Freiburg), after long negotiations, a peace of unlimited duration was signed. On one side were the 13 cantons of the Confederation and their allies (the Abbot and the city of St. Gallen, the Three Leagues/Graubünden, Valais, Mulhouse), and on the other Francis I in his capacity as King of France and Duke of Milan. The Confederation in practice gave up its claim to a protectorate over Milan. The parties undertook not to support each other's enemies and to settle disputes by arbitration or through the courts. France was to pay war indemnities of 400,000 crowns for the Dijon campaign and 300,000 crowns for the Milan campaign. It offered a further 300,000 crowns in return for the cession of the transalpine bailiwicks (except Bellinzona), but the Swiss rejected this. An annual pension of 2,000 francs was granted to each canton, to Valais and to the Three Leagues. The commercial privileges of the Swiss in Milan and at the Lyon fair were renewed, and mutual free trade was recognised.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. Because the cession of the bailiwicks was rejected, the territory of the later canton of Ticino remained definitively with the Confederation; Mendrisio did so too from 1521. In political weight, the peace was equal to the Ewige Richtung (Perpetual Accord) concluded with Austria in 1511. In 2025 two copies of the treaty were inscribed on the UNESCO Memory of the World Register.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. The peace was the result of the defeat at Marignano on 13-14 September 1515 and of the Gallarate negotiations (1515) that followed. It was an important step on the road to the mercenary alliance with France of 1521. The orientation toward France became one of the constants of Swiss foreign relations and domestic politics until the French Revolution.", "8bc08d8b"]},
"antlasma10-habsburg-baris-1562":{"baslik": ["Renewal of peace with the Habsburgs (1562) — after the conflict over Transylvania and Temesvár, the earlier undertakings were mutually reaffirmed", "7b018bec"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. In the month of Ramadan of the Hijri year 969 (May 1562), it was decided in Istanbul to continue the peace with the Habsburgs. According to TDV, this agreement mutually renewed the earlier undertakings of the two sides; that is, it was a return to the framework of the peace of 1547. In the five-year agreement reached in 1547, Ferdinand had accepted to pay tribute to the Ottomans for the Hungarian lands he held; the Habsburgs called this payment a 'gift of honour' (munus honoriarium). Britannica too writes that in 1562 Ferdinand established an uneasy peace by undertaking to pay tribute to the sultan for the part of Hungary that remained with Austria. The sources consulted do not give the amount of the tribute or the duration of the peace.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. The peace closed the breach in relations that had occurred when a Habsburg army entered Transylvania in 1551. In response to this incursion, the Ottomans took Temesvár in 1552 (26 July 1552) and in August 1552 established a province (beylerbeylik) centred on Temesvár; Lugoj and Sebeș were also conquered in the same summer. The peace left these gains in place. The eight-year embassy of Busbecq, the Habsburg ambassador who had been in Istanbul since 1554, also came to an end in 1562; the ambassador returned to Vienna at the end of the same year.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. TDV emphasises that the agreement brought no solution to the disturbances along the frontiers. When Ferdinand died in 1564 and was succeeded by Maximilian II, incidents in Transylvania increased again, and the struggle came to be concentrated on the fortresses of Tokaj and Pankota. This tension opened the way to the Hungarian campaign of 1566, Suleiman the Magnificent's last campaign, which was aimed at Szigetvár and Eger.", "6fd75c0c"]},
"antlasma10-ikinci-thorn-1466":{"baslik": ["Second Treaty of Thorn (Toruń) (1466) — Royal Prussia was attached to Poland, and the Teutonic Order became Poland's vassal", "c5edb6e4"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. By the treaty signed on 19 October 1466 at Toruń (Thorn) between King Casimir IV of Poland and the Order of the Teutonic Knights, the Order ceded to Poland Eastern Pomerania (Pomerelia, including Gdańsk/Danzig), the lands on both sides of the Vistula, the Chełmno (Kulm) region and the bishopric of Warmia (Ermland). These lands passed directly under the sovereignty of the Polish Crown and took the name 'Royal Prussia'. The rest of Prussia, east of the Vistula, remained with the Order; for these lands, however, the Grand Master became a vassal of the Polish king. The Order, which until then had consisted only of Germans, was also compelled to admit Polish members.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. Britannica regards the treaty as Casimir IV's greatest achievement: Gdańsk Pomerania, granted broad autonomy, was reunited with Poland, and Poland gained an outlet to the Baltic. The eastern part centred on Königsberg, that is, the later East Prussia, remained in the Order's hands as a vassal state with its own diet and administration. Thus a Polish-speaking wedge came between German-speaking East Prussia and the German lands.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. The Thirteen Years' War (1454-1466) began with the revolt of the people of Prussia and of the Order's own vassals against their masters. When the rebels asked Casimir IV for help in 1454, Poland declared war on the Order; Casimir won a decisive victory at Puck in 1462. The Order, which had been in decline since Grunwald in 1410, lost its independence in Prussia with this treaty. The relationship of vassalage that was established prepared the ground for the last Grand Master, Albert, to turn the Order's state into a secular duchy subject to Poland in 1525.", "28992f13"]},
"antlasma10-krakow-1525":{"baslik": ["Peace of Kraków (1525) — the state of the Teutonic Order became the secular Duchy of Prussia, subject to Poland", "14a028df"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. By the peace signed at Kraków on 8 April 1525 through the mediation of the rulers of Brandenburg-Ansbach and Liegnitz (Legnica), the state of the Order of the Teutonic Knights in Prussia was turned into a secular and hereditary duchy subject to Poland. Prussia was given as a fief of the Polish Crown to the last Grand Master, Albert of Brandenburg, and his male line, and after them to his three brothers and their male lines. Albert resigned the office of Grand Master; on 10 April, the second day of Easter, he received his fief in a ceremony at Kraków and swore an oath of allegiance to King Sigismund I of Poland. When he returned to Prussia, the diet that met at Königsberg swore allegiance to him in the presence of the Polish envoys.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to the New German Biography (NDB), from this date the Teutonic Order ceased to exist in Prussia; Albert, following Luther's advice, converted to Protestantism. The imperial ban (Reichsacht) proclaimed by Emperor Charles V remained without effect. In 1526 the Order moved its headquarters to Mergentheim in Franconia. According to Britannica, this duchy united with the Electorate of Brandenburg in 1618 and became the foundation for the rise of the Hohenzollern monarchy as a great power; Polish suzerainty was ended by the Peace of Oliva in 1660. The Kraków formula was also taken as a model in 1561 for the Duchy of Courland in Livonia.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. The Grand Master had been a vassal of the Polish king since the Second Treaty of Thorn of 1466; Albert refused to take the oath of allegiance. His seizure of Braunsberg (Braniewo) in Warmia by a surprise attack on New Year's Day 1520 started the 'Horsemen's War' (Reiterkrieg); the war devastated the country but produced no result. In 1521, through the mediation of the emperor and the King of Hungary, a four-year armistice lasting until 4 April 1525 was concluded. When the recourse to arbitration and the search for help from the Empire came to nothing, the proposal of secularisation was accepted as the armistice was expiring.", "49ba86d5"]},
"antlasma10-lausanne-1564":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Lausanne (1564) — Savoy definitively renounced Vaud; Gex, Chablais, Ternier and Gaillard were returned to Savoy", "4c138878"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. On 30 October 1564 at Lausanne, after eight days of negotiations mediated by the Swiss cantons, a treaty was signed between Bern and Duke Emmanuel Philibert of Savoy. The duke definitively renounced his rights over Vaud. In return, the Pays de Gex, Chablais and the bailiwicks of Ternier and Gaillard were to be given back to him on 1 March 1565. The parties handed over to each other the documents relating to the regions concerned. The treaty also contained a clause of religious toleration for the Reformed in the regions returned.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. The treaty made permanent the division of the lands that Bern had taken from Savoy in 1536: Vaud remained Swiss, while the southern shore of Lake Geneva and Gex returned to Savoy. Implementation was delayed, however. When the kings of France and Spain did not grant the unconditional recognition that Bern wanted, Bern did not hand over the lands, and it did not withdraw from the places it occupied until August 1567.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. Emmanuel Philibert, who had recovered his duchy through the treaties of Cateau-Cambrésis of 1559, demanded the return of the places the Swiss had taken in 1476 (Bex, Aigle) and in 1536. He chose diplomacy instead of war and won over the Catholic cantons and the kings of France and Spain. Negotiations were held at Neuchâtel in 1560-1561, at Basel in 1563 and at Nyon in 1564. Although an edict permitting Reformed worship was issued in 1569, the toleration clause was not observed. The last places remaining in Swiss hands returned to Savoy by the Treaty of Thonon of 1569. Because the treaty did not mention the bishopric of Lausanne, even in 1582 the Pope and the Emperor were pressing the Catholic cantons to support the titular bishop's claim to recover the lordship of Lausanne.", "42e698b9"]},
"antlasma10-lublin-1569":{"baslik": ["Union of Lublin (1569) — Poland and Lithuania were united in a single state, and the Ukrainian lands passed to the Polish Crown", "846ba82d"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. The union signed on 1 July 1569 at a joint diet of Polish and Lithuanian representatives at Lublin brought the two countries together in a single federative state, the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth (Rzeczpospolita). The titles of King of Poland and Grand Duke of Lithuania were combined in a single jointly elected ruler; a common diet (Sejm) and senate, a common foreign policy and a single monetary system were established. The Grand Duchy of Lithuania retained its own laws, administration, courts, army and treasury. In territorial terms, the Polish Crown received the regions of Podlasie, Volhynia, Podolia, Bratslav and Kiev; the nobles of these lands were granted the rights of the Polish nobility. Apart from its own territory, Lithuania was left with the regions of Belarus, Brest and Pinsk.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. In a union that on paper consisted of two equal partners, Poland, which kept the lands it had seized and was more strongly represented in the diet, became the dominant side. Most of the Ukrainian lands thus came under direct Polish administration. The united state remained one of the great powers of Eastern Europe until the partitions at the end of the 18th century.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. The two countries, which had shared the same ruler since the Union of Krewo of 1385, faced the danger of separating upon the death of Sigismund II Augustus, since he had no heir. In the Livonian War, which began in 1558, the lesser nobility of Lithuania, under threat from Muscovy, wanted unification, while the great families resisted. When the diet that met in January 1569 could not reach agreement, the king annexed Podlasie and Volhynia (including Kiev and Bratslav) to Poland; the Lithuanian nobles, unable to risk war, returned to the table in June. According to TDV, the intensive policy of assimilation pursued after the union was one of the factors that paved the way for the emergence of the Cossacks on the stage of history in Ukraine.", "f495c2d5"]},
"antlasma10-lyon-1601":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Lyon (1601) — France gave up Saluzzo and in return received Bresse, Bugey, Valromey and Gex from Savoy", "7f081ff5"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. The treaty signed at Lyon on 17 January 1601 resolved the dispute over Saluzzo between King Henry IV of France and Duke Charles Emmanuel I of Savoy. France renounced its right to the Marquisate of Saluzzo, an enclave within Savoyard territory. In return it received from Savoy Bresse, Bugey and Valromey, as well as the Pays de Gex, which at that time had been administered by Geneva for about ten years. The Duke of Savoy for his part recovered the Gaillard district occupied by Geneva, as well as Chablais, Faucigny and the Genevois. Britannica summarises this outcome as a peace in which the duke was obliged to leave three of the dynasty's territories to France and in return was allowed to keep Saluzzo.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to the HLS article 'Bresse', the region became a territory that Henry IV conquered despite one of the most powerful fortresses in Europe, at Bourg, and that was exchanged for Saluzzo and permanently attached to France. The surrender of Saluzzo, however, weakened French influence in Italy. For Geneva the result was severe: its hopes of territorial expansion came to an end, and for the first time the city had a common border with France in Gex.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. Saluzzo, a French possession since 1529, had been occupied by Charles Emmanuel in 1588, when France was shaken by the Wars of Religion, and he had also entered Provence. The Peace of Vervins of 1598 referred the question to papal arbitration; when no solution emerged, Henry IV went to war and reconquered Bresse. The duke, obliged to cede territory to France, turned his attention to Geneva, but his attempted surprise attack in December 1602 (the Escalade) failed.", "e9a993a0"]},
"antlasma10-moskova-litvanya-1503":{"baslik": ["Muscovite-Lithuanian Truce (1503) — the Chernigov-Seversk land and the Oka basin passed to Moscow", "b4daf887"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. The agreement made in the spring of 1503 between Grand Prince Ivan III of Moscow and Alexander, Grand Duke of Lithuania and King of Poland, halted the war that had begun in 1500 not with a lasting peace but with a truce for a fixed term. According to the Lithuanian Encyclopaedia (VLE), under the truce Moscow gained the last principalities in the upper Oka basin (Mosalsk, Mtsensk, Lyubutsk), the lands of Chernigov, Starodub, Gomel and Novgorod-Seversk, and parts of the principalities of Mstislavl, Smolensk and Kiev. The area lost amounted to more than a quarter of the territory of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania. The city of Smolensk, however, remained with Lithuania.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to the Encyclopedia of Ukraine, Chernigov, which had been attached to Lithuania since the second half of the 14th century, passed to Moscow in 1503 together with the whole Chernigov-Seversk land; Novgorod-Severskiy, which had been Lithuanian since 1356, was also attached to Moscow in the same year. These lands did not return to Poland-Lithuania until the Truce of Deulino in 1618. Britannica emphasises Ivan III's aim of recovering from Poland-Lithuania his 'ancestral lands' in Ukraine; but it adds that the war did not end as decisively as he had expected and that most of Ukraine still remained in the hands of his rival.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. After the peace of 1494, Ivan III again attacked Lithuania's eastern lands in 1500; in the same year Prince Semyon of Chernigov, who was in Lithuanian service, went over to Moscow. On 14 July 1500 the Lithuanian army was defeated on the Vedrosha; Lithuania could neither draw Livonia to its side nor obtain military aid from the West. Because of the lands lost under the truce, war broke out again in 1507 and continued, with intermittent armistices, until 1537; in 1514 Smolensk too passed to Moscow.", "b6ac7dd7"]},
"antlasma10-pisa-piombino-1399":{"baslik": ["The sale of Pisa to the Visconti (1399) — Gherardo Appiani gave up Pisa for 200,000 florins and reserved the lordship of Piombino for himself", "072fb98c"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. On 13 February 1399 the agreement between the lord of Pisa, Gherardo Leonardo Appiani, and the Duke of Milan, Gian Galeazzo Visconti, was concluded. Appiani ceded Pisa to Visconti for 200,000 florins. He reserved for himself the lordship of Piombino: Piombino, Suvereto, Buriano and Scarlino, together with the islands of Elba, Pianosa, Montecristo, Cerboli and Palmaiola. To avoid disorder, the city was formally handed over to the duke's representative, Antoniolo Porro, only a few days later. Three days after the handover, Appiani set sail for his new state. Emperor Wenceslaus (Venceslao) approved the agreement, recognised the establishment of the state of Piombino and invested Appiani with this state with the title of count (investiture).\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. The agreement gave birth to a small state on the Tuscan coast: the Piombino of the Appiani family. Pisa, for its part, passed into the hands of Milan; Florence had tried to prevent this but had failed. To strengthen his small state, Appiani bought the castles of Montione and Valle. He also forged a tie with the powerful Roman family of the Colonna by marrying Paola Colonna, sister of the later Pope Martin V.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. Gherardo, who had become sole lord of Pisa on the death of his father Iacopo, saw that he could not preserve his sovereignty between hostile Florence and a Visconti who wanted the city at all costs, and secretly opened negotiations for its sale. The commissioners sent by Florence tried to wreck the bargaining; Appiani sent them back, and on 21 January 1399 he took the government into his own hands alone and had the Pisans he suspected killed or imprisoned. Piombino repelled two Genoese attacks in 1401-1402. On 3 June 1403 it came under the protection of Florence as a 'raccomandato' (protégé).", "8366433c"]},
"antlasma10-stettin-1570":{"baslik": ["Peace of Stettin (1570) — the Northern Seven Years' War ended without any change of borders, and Sweden undertook to pay a ransom for Älvsborg", "be703392"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. By the peace signed at Stettin on 13 December 1570 between Denmark-Norway and Sweden, the two sides renounced their mutual claims, and the places seized in the war were returned. Sweden gave back to Denmark-Norway the Norwegian provinces of Jämtland and Härjedalen, which it had occupied in 1564, and formally renounced its claim to Gotland. For the fortress of Älvsborg, which Denmark had taken in 1563, Sweden undertook to pay a ransom of 150,000 thalers within three years. Frederick II formally renounced his claim to the Swedish throne, and John III his claim to the throne of Denmark-Norway. Dutch, French, Polish and Saxon mediators played a part in the negotiations.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. The longest and most destructive war fought between Denmark and Sweden up to that time ended in a draw, and there was no change in the borders. Nevertheless, the terms were in favour of Denmark-Norway; the large war indemnity in particular was counted as its gain. The fact that Sweden had to pay a ransom to recover Älvsborg, its only outlet to the sea in the west, showed what the war had cost Sweden.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. Behind the war lay the rivalry over the Baltic provinces and the Russian trade, Sweden's wish to break its encirclement by Denmark-Norway, and Eric XIV's three-crowns coat of arms and his claims to Skåne, Halland and Gotland. Eric's deposition in 1568 and the Russian tsar's attack on Swedish lands opened the way to peace. After the war, John III pursued an aggressive Baltic policy, while Frederick II, whose treasury had been emptied, followed a peaceful course for the rest of his reign. In Norway, Sarpsborg, which had been burned during the war, was moved and took the name Fredrikstad.", "77140586"]},
"antlasma10-stolbova-1617":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Stolbovo (1617) — Sweden took Ingria and Kexholm, and Russia was cut off from the Baltic", "7ba4ef22"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. By the peace signed on 27 February 1617 (Julian; 9 March Gregorian) in the village of Stolbovo, south of Lake Ladoga, which no longer exists today, Sweden gave back to Moscow Novgorod and the other places it had occupied in northern Russia; in return it kept Ingria, together with Nöteborg (today's Shlisselburg), and the Kexholm (Karelia) region. Russia was to pay Sweden an indemnity of 20,000 rubles. The Swedish prince Charles Philip renounced his claim to the Russian throne. For purposes of trade, the Russians would be able to open trading houses in Stockholm, Reval (Tallinn) and Vyborg, and the Swedes in Novgorod, Moscow and Pskov.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. The treaty cut Russia off completely from the Baltic Sea; this situation lasted until the Great Northern War (1700-1721). Sweden, for its part, was unable to realise its hope of gaining control of Russia's trade with Western Europe by closing off the White Sea route. The Orthodox population of Kexholm emigrated in large numbers, and Finnish peasants settled in their place.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. During the Time of Troubles (1606-1613), the leading men of Moscow, shaken by a succession crisis, war with Poland and peasant uprisings, offered the throne in 1610 to Władysław, son of King Sigismund III of Poland. Sweden, which was at war with Poland at the time, responded by declaring war on Russia and put forward its own candidate for the throne, Charles Philip. Having recovered under the tsardom of Michael Romanov from 1613, Moscow saved Novgorod by making this peace with Sweden and was able to turn all its strength against Poland.", "32bc94a0"]},
"antlasma10-tordesillas-1494":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Tordesillas (1494) — Spain and Portugal divided the new lands in the ocean by a pole-to-pole line running 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands", "e313b122"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. On 7 June 1494, in the Castilian town of Tordesillas, the representatives of the Catholic Monarchs Ferdinand and Isabella and the envoys of King John II of Portugal agreed that, for the lands discovered and yet to be discovered in the ocean, a straight line should be drawn from the North Pole to the South Pole 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands. The islands and mainland east of the line were to belong to Portugal, and those west of it to Castile and Aragon. The parties undertook not to send ships into the other half, not to seek or take land there, and to hand over to the other side anything found there by mistake. To determine the line, joint ships carrying pilots, astronomers and sailors from both sides were to assemble at Gran Canaria within ten months and sail westward from Cape Verde. Spanish ships were granted free passage through the Portuguese half; for places that the Spaniards might find up to 20 June, it was further agreed that the first 250 leagues would belong to Portugal and the remaining 120 leagues to Spain.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to Britannica, the line corresponds to about 46°30' west longitude today. The new boundary ensured that the coast of Brazil, which Pedro Álvares Cabral reached in 1500, fell to Portugal; in later centuries Portuguese settlement extending far west of the line became the basis of Brazil's claims to the interior. The other European states that ventured into the Atlantic never recognised this division.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. After Columbus's discoveries, the Spanish-born Pope Alexander VI had, with the bull Inter caetera of 1493, placed the boundary 100 leagues west of Cape Verde. When John II objected, concerned that not enough room would be left at sea for the African voyages, the line was moved west at Tordesillas; Pope Julius II approved the change in 1506. After Magellan's expedition, the parties made a second division, in Asia as well, with the Treaty of Zaragoza of 1529.", "956d6598"]},
"antlasma10-vervins-1598":{"baslik": ["Peace of Vervins (1598) — Spain returned the French towns it had occupied, including Calais, and recognised Henry IV as king", "5238f7ba"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. On 2 May 1598 at Vervins, near the border of the Spanish Netherlands, peace was signed between France and Spain (with its ally Savoy) through the mediation of Alessandro de' Medici, legate of Pope Clement VIII; the negotiations were conducted for France by Pomponne de Bellièvre and for Spain by Archduke Albert. The treaty largely renewed the settlement of Cateau-Cambrésis of 1559: towns taken in the war were returned. Spain gave back to France Calais, Ardres and Doullens, which it held in Picardy and on the northern frontier, and Blavet in Brittany; Cambrai, however, remained with Spain. France's claim to the Marquisate of Saluzzo, which Duke Charles Emmanuel I of Savoy had seized in 1588, was referred to the arbitration of the pope. Spain recognised Henry IV's right to the French throne. The Swiss cantons and their allies were also included in the peace.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to Britannica, Vervins and the Edict of Nantes of the same year together ended the French Wars of Religion. Spain had failed to achieve its highest aims and had returned to its position of 1559; nevertheless, at the end of the century it remained the greatest power in Europe. The peace is regarded rather as a diplomatic success for France. HLS writes that this peace, made between exhausted armies, remained unstable, and that in the following years Franco-Spanish rivalry was carried into Switzerland in the diplomatic sphere.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. After Henry IV converted to Catholicism in 1593 and broke up the Catholic League, he declared war on Spain on 17 January 1595. The Spaniards took Doullens and Calais, and on 11 March 1597 they surprised and seized Amiens; Henry recaptured Amiens on 25 September 1597 after a six-month siege, and in March 1598 the last League resistance in Brittany collapsed. Philip II, who had declared bankruptcy in 1596, turned toward peace. When the Saluzzo question remained unresolved, war broke out between France and Savoy in 1600 and was ended by the Treaty of Lyon of 1601; Geneva, however, did not join this settlement until the Peace of Saint-Julien of 1603.", "97b8db9e"]},
"antlasma10-vilnius-1561":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Vilnius (1561) — the Livonian Confederation was dissolved, and the Duchy of Courland became a vassal of Poland-Lithuania", "f2315f7f"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. By the agreements made at Vilnius (Wilna) in November 1561, Gotthard Kettler, the last Master of the Teutonic Order in Livonia, and Archbishop Wilhelm of Riga, of the House of Hohenzollern, submitted to Sigismund II Augustus, King of Poland and Grand Duke of Lithuania. By the Pacta subiectionis (Provisio ducalis) of 28 November 1561, following the Prussian model, a duchy held in fief was carved out of southern Livonia for Kettler: Kettler and all his male descendants, as vassals of the Polish king, received the title of duke, a coat of arms and privileges; the borders of the duchy were described point by point in the document. The Privilegium Sigismundi Augusti of the same date granted the Livonian nobles full ownership of the fiefs they held and over their peasants; the Lutheran church order was guaranteed. Livonia proper came under the protection of Lithuania.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. The treaty is the formal end of the medieval Livonian Confederation. It was put into effect at Riga on 5 March 1562: Kettler resigned as Master and took the title of duke, and the Order in Livonia was secularised. The Duchy of Courland and Semigallia remained a vassal of Poland-Lithuania until the third partition in 1795. The city of Riga, however, did not join this arrangement; for a short time it remained an independent city-state and did not pass to Poland until 1581.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. In 1558 Ivan IV invaded Livonia, took Narva and Dorpat and besieged Reval. Unable to withstand the Russian attack, the knights dissolved the Order, and its lands were divided: Livonia passed under Lithuanian protection, Courland to Poland, Estonia to Sweden and Ösel (Saaremaa) to Denmark. The Livonian War thus turned into a war in which Russia sought to hold on to its conquests against Sweden and Lithuania. After the Union of Lublin of 1569, the duchy was attached to the new Commonwealth (Rzeczpospolita) by a document of 3 August 1569.", "f78c128c"]},
"antlasma10-yam-zapolski-1582":{"baslik": ["Truce of Yam Zapolsky (1582) — Muscovy gave up Livonia, and Polotsk remained with Poland-Lithuania", "fac40b6d"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. The negotiations were conducted at Kiverova Gorka, between Pskov and Yam Zapolsky, and the truce was signed on 15 January 1582. The Tsardom of Muscovy renounced all its claims to Livonia and ceded the land of Polotsk, together with certain fortresses, to the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. According to Britannica, Russia also gave back all the Lithuanian territory it had seized. The truce was concluded for ten years.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. Yam Zapolsky closed the Russo-Polish front of the Livonian War, which had been going on since 1558. The struggle of more than twenty years that Ivan the Terrible had begun in order to gain access to the Baltic ended in disappointment: Livonia remained with Poland-Lithuania and northern Estonia with Sweden. By the peace made with Sweden the following year (1583), Russia also lost several of its towns on the shore of the Gulf of Finland, and with them its only outlet to the Baltic, and gave up its claim to Estonia.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. In 1577 Ivan the Terrible invaded and plundered Livonia. King Stephen Báthory raised an army with taxes he wrested from the diet and went over to the counter-offensive: in 1579 he took Polotsk after a short siege, in the autumn of 1580 the fortress of Velikiye Luki fell, and then Pskov was besieged. At the same time Sweden too attacked Livonia, took many fortresses and cut the Russians off from the Narva trade. The papal envoy, whom the tsar had been keeping in play with the possibility of a union of the Eastern and Western churches, worked against the continuation of the war; hard pressed on two fronts, the tsar began to seek a truce.", "65600f8b"]},
"antlasma10-zadar-1358":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Zadar (1358) — Venice withdrew from Dalmatia, and Dubrovnik passed under the protection of the Hungarian-Croatian crown", "731cda92"], "metin": ["PROVISIONS. By the peace signed on 18 February 1358 at Zadar, in the sacristy of the church of the monastery of St Francis (Sveti Frane), between King Louis I (of Anjou) of Hungary and Croatia and the Republic of Venice, Venice renounced all its rights over Dalmatia, stretching from the middle of the Gulf of Kvarner to the region of Durrës (Durazzo). Among the places renounced were listed the cities of Nin, Zadar, Skradin, Šibenik, Trogir, Split and Dubrovnik and the islands of Osor, Cres, Krk, Rab, Pag, Brač, Hvar and Korčula. The Venetian doge was to give up the title 'Duke of Dalmatia and Croatia', and Venetian forces were to evacuate Dalmatia within 22 days. In return, Louis gave back the Treviso region, which he had seized in 1356, Cividale and his lands in Istria; prisoners were released, and freedom of movement was again recognised for the people of both countries.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. Dubrovnik had been under Venetian overlordship since 1205; Venice appointed the city's knez (count) and archbishop and controlled its trade. After the Peace of Zadar, in May 1358, the people of Dubrovnik and Korčula expelled the Venetian counts, and by the Treaty of Višegrad of 27 May 1358 Dubrovnik recognised the overlordship of the Hungarian-Croatian king. Over time the city, acquiring attributes of statehood such as territory, a coat of arms, a flag and a coinage, began to call itself the 'Respublica Ragusina'.\n\nCAUSES AND CONSEQUENCES. The treaty was the product of the war against Venice that Louis began in 1356. The Dalmatian cities then experienced an economic and cultural flourishing for about half a century; this period closed when Venice regained control of Dalmatia between 1409 and 1420. Dubrovnik, however, did not return to Venice but continued on its own path as a republic.", "a23601e4"]},
"ayan-hangi-aile-nereyi-tutuyordu":{"baslik": ["Which Ayan Held Which Region? Families with a Documented Record and Their Territories", "39a6ce72"], "metin": ["The rule of the list. Each line below has a documented reference; no family without a source has been written. The backbone is the breakdown of families by region in the TDV article `âyan` (Özcan Mert, 1991); for those that have their own articles, that article's reference has also been given. Another backbone of the same list appears in the TDV article `Selim III`: Selim III “had to put up with powerful ayan such as Pazvandoğlu, Tirsiniklioğlu, Tepedelenli, İşkodralı, Canikli, Cezzar and Kavalalı.”\n\nRUMELIA. ① Tirsiniklioğlu İsmail Agha — ayan of Rusçuk (d. 1806; it is recorded that in his youth he worked as a water carrier, and that in 1792, at the head of a band of sixty men, he roamed the areas of Sistova, Nikopol, Tarnovo, Hezargrad, Pleven and Lovech; TDV `tirsiniklioglu-ismail-aga`). ② Alemdar Mustafa Pasha — ayan of Hezargrad, then of Rusçuk, “ayan of ayans”, governor of Silistra and commander-in-chief of the Danube; the only ayan in Ottoman history to rise to the grand vizierate (TDV `alemdar-mustafa-pasa`). ③ Pazvandoğlu Osman — in the TDV Encyclopaedia's own definition, “the rebel ayan of the Vidin and Northern Bulgaria region” (d. 1807; his family was of Tuzla-Bosnian origin; TDV `pazvandoglu-osman`). ④ Ali Pasha of Tepelena (Tepedelenli) — Ioannina and its surroundings, “governor of Ioannina” in the TDV heading (d. 1822; his date of birth is disputed: 1740 or 1744, and 1750-51 in sources of the period; TDV `tepedelenli-ali-pasa`). ⑤ İşkodralı (the Bushatis) — Shkodra; no separate TDV article could be found, and its reference is the `selim-iii` sentence above. ⑥ From among those who personally signed the Sened-i İttifak: İsmail Bey of Serres (Siroz) and Mustafa Bey, mutasarrıf (district governor) of Çirmen; among those who took part in the negotiations were Hacıahmedoğlu Seyyid İbrahim Agha, voivode of Bolu, Ahmed Agha, ayan of Şile, and Kalyoncu Mustafa Bey, ayan of Bilecik (TDV `sened-i-ittifak`; the Kalyoncuoğulları in Bilecik, `ayan`).\n\nANATOLIA. ⑦ The Karaosmanoğulları — Manisa and its surroundings, “an ayan family influential in the Western Anatolia region in the 18th century”; when the founder, Kara Osman Agha, died in 1706, he left behind a family that would take on a role in the administration of the region (TDV `karaosmanogullari`, Yuzo Nagata). The Sened-i İttifak was signed by Karaosmanoğlu Ömer Agha. ⑧ The Çapanoğulları (Cebbarzades) — “Central Anatolia, with Bozok as its center”; Ahmed Agha was voivode of the Mamalu Türkmens in 1732 and of Bozok in 1741, and when in 1755 he obtained the voivodeship of Bozok as a malikâne (life-term tax farm) he “became the undisputed master of the region” (TDV `capanogullari`, Özcan Mert). The Deed was signed by Çapanoğlu Süleyman Bey. ⑨ Canikli Hacı Ali Pasha and his sons — Samsun and its surroundings (`ayan`; `selim-iii`). ⑩ The Tuzcuoğulları — the eastern Black Sea, centered on Rize; the earliest record is from 1763, from the 1760s they took over the office of ayan of Rize, and in 1806 Memiş Agha became warden of the fortress of Faş (Poti) (TDV `tuzcuogullari`, Mehmet Hacısalihoğlu). ⑪ The Kozanoğulları — Kozan/Çukurova; their origin is traced to Türkmen tribes that migrated to Cilicia (TDV `kozanogullari`). ⑫ The others counted in the same sentence of the `âyan` article, with their regions: the Zennecizades in Kayseri, the Müderriszades in Ankara, Kanlızade in Balıkesir, the Katiboğulları in İzmir, the Yılanlıoğulları in Isparta, the Tekelioğulları in Antalya, and the Menemencioğulları in Çukurova.\n\nARAB provinces. ⑬ The Azmzades (al-Azm) — Syria; a family “originally from Konya which later settled in Syria”, which held the posts of governor, mutasarrıf and commander of the pilgrimage (emirü'l-hac) (TDV `azmzadeler`, Ali Karaca). ⑭ The Babanzades (Babans) — northern Iraq (TDV `ayan`). ⑮ Cezzar Ahmed Pasha — Acre/Sidon (TDV `cezzar-ahmed-pasa`; `selim-iii` counts him among the powerful ayan). ⑯ Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala — Egypt (TDV `kavalali-mehmed-ali-pasa`; in the same `selim-iii` sentence).\n\nTHE LIMITS of the list. This is not an exhaustive inventory but a set of families for which a documented reference exists: even the TDV article `âyan` opens its own list with “Among these… had won fame”, that is, it says that it is giving a sample. At the district (kaza) scale there were hundreds of ayan (the ayan of 41 kazas subject to Tirsiniklioğlu alone have been counted — Bayrak 2009, p. 64), and their names have not been compiled with references at this scale.", "4cca4231"], "bag": ["The geographical pattern is no coincidence: in Rumelia the great armed ayan stand out (Pazvandoğlu, Tepedelenli, Tirsiniklioğlu), and in Anatolia the families linked to the center through life-term tax farms and voivodeships (Karaosmanoğulları, Çapanoğulları). Nagehan Üstündağ Özdemir records this difference explicitly: “The stages of development of the ayan in the Balkans and in Anatolia show some minor differences”, and these differences explain why the Mountain Bandit (Dağlı) revolts that engulfed the Balkans in the 18th century and the “type of the mighty ayan” were more pronounced in that region.", "af319d2f"]},
"ayanlik-isyan-mi-ozerklik-mi":{"baslik": ["Was the ayan system rebellion, autonomy or corrupt provincial government — and who appointed the ayan to office?", "c4f4f44d"], "ozet": ["One and the same institution can be read as three different things: an armed force opposed to the centre, a recognised form of local autonomy, or a decayed form of state office. The sources do not choose one of these three readings — depending on which ayan, which region and which year you look at, all three find support. There is also a more concrete question: who put people into this position — the sultan, the people, or whoever could seize it?", "ff1fe36a"], "metin": ["First, a definition: what the ayan were not. The article `âyan` in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam (by Özcan Mert) says that the word was used very broadly: \"The local dynasties that appear in historical documents as voyvoda, mütesellim, muhassıl, mutasarrıf and governor are at the same time also referred to by the terms ayan, derebeyi (lord of the valley) or mütegallibe (usurper).\" In other words, the same person could be mentioned in a document sometimes by an official title and sometimes by an epithet meaning \"strongman\" — the term itself carries a judgement. In the classical period the influence of an ayan \"did not extend beyond the limits of the settlement in which he lived\"; the institution began to grow from the second half of the sixteenth century onwards.\n\nWho appointed them to OFFICE? The answer changes over time, and TDV dates it step by step: the chief ayan (reîs-i a'yân) was at first determined by election — according to the documents, \"for the first time in the 1680s\" — and the person chosen had to \"obtain a collective petition (mahzar) from the people, a written attestation (ilâm) from the kadi and a written order (buyruldu) from the governor\"; in return for the order a fee called \"âyâniye\" was paid. Then governors began \"to issue orders appointing ayan in return for bribes\", and the centre intervened: in April 1765 governors were forbidden to issue such orders and an investigation by the grand vizier was made a condition; in January 1769, because of the war, \"a system of appointing ayan by popular election was adopted\"; in April 1779 there was a return to the procedure of 1765; in May 1784 the rule that \"no one could become chief ayan without a letter from the grand vizier\" was renewed; in 1786 the Sublime Porte put an end to the office of ayan altogether and replaced it with that of town steward (şehir kethüdâsı); in November 1790 the old arrangement returned, and ayan were appointed to office by a \"sharia court certificate (hüccet-i şer'iyye)\". So all three answers are correct, but in different years: the sultan or central government gives approval, the people and local notables elect, and in practice the office often goes to \"whoever grabs it\". Hereditary succession, for its part, came through a separate channel: because after 1695 certain tax-revenue sources (mukataa) were farmed out for life under the \"malikâne\" system, the ayan gained \"the opportunity to keep their sources of revenue going by inheritance\"; by the middle of the eighteenth century \"great families with practically the character of a dynasty\" had emerged. The guarantees given by the Deed of Alliance (Sened-i İttifak) likewise applied to the ayan \"themselves and, after their death, their children and dynasties\" — that is, the state had put de facto heredity into writing.\n\nThe FIRST reading — rebellion. The epithet TDV uses to describe some ayan says this directly: the heading of the article on Pazvandoğlu Osman calls him \"the rebel ayan of Vidin and the region of northern Bulgaria\"; to suppress his revolt Selim III mobilised \"as if waging war on a foreign state\", and still could not get rid of him (`selim-iii`). In the Second Edirne Incident of 1806, the ayan of Rumelia took up arms against the establishment of the New Order (Nizâm-ı Cedîd) army in Rumelia and forced the sultan to back down (`ayan`, `kabakci-isyani`).\n\nThe SECOND reading — recognised autonomy or partnership. The same ayan were officials of the same state: they collected taxes, supplied the army with soldiers and grain, sent meat to Istanbul, procured saltpetre for the gunpowder works and caught bandits (`ayan`). Nagehan Üstündağ Özdemir describes this relationship as \"a conflict involving mutual dependence\": the centre depends on the ayan for taxes, public order and soldiers, while the ayan need the centre's approval \"to receive administrative authority and ranks, to protect their own spheres of control against rivals and to prove their legitimacy\". Ali Yaycıoğlu's Partners of the Empire (2016) takes this reading a step further: according to a review that reports the book's argument, Yaycıoğlu regards the Deed of Alliance neither as \"a late Magna Carta that was a step toward progressive constitutionalism\" nor as \"a sign of political centralisation\"; in his words, the deed envisaged \"a horizontal partnership of elites, bearing full responsibility, whose security was mutually guaranteed\" (as reported in Bostan 2017, pp. 157-158).\n\nThe THIRD reading — corrupt provincial government. The source says this too, in its own words: while carrying out their duties the ayan \"very often also pursued their own interests, rightly or wrongly\", and struggles over the office of ayan turned \"from time to time into bloody clashes\"; it was observed that after Çapanoğlu Mustafa acquired the office of voyvoda of Bozok as a lifetime tax farm, he \"at times attempted to oppress the people\" (`capanogullari`). The state's own view is also close to this third reading: an archive-based study describing the practice of confiscation records that the state was \"of the opinion that the ayan generally amassed their wealth by illegitimate means\" and for this reason tended to seize their property (Bayrak 2009, p. 69).\n\nThis card passes no VERDICT. All three readings are measurable faces of the same institution; which of them comes to the fore varies with the region (Rumelia or Anatolia), the year (before 1765, 1786, or after 1790) and the individual. The outcome, however, was written by the centre: Mahmud II established central authority \"by sending armies against rebellious ayan and sometimes by setting them against one another\"; against those who were obedient and powerful he did not use force; \"he waited for them to die and then reduced their influence in the provinces by appointing in their place not their heirs but representatives of the Sublime Porte\" (`ayan`).", "9727a98b"], "bag": ["View: The Deed of Alliance was a constitutional moment — a document resembling Magna Carta, by which the ayan obtained rights against the sultan. Counter-view (TDV `sened-i-ittifak`, Ali Akyıldız): the document is a text that \"arose under the pressure of historical events and circumstances\" and \"is not, as constitutional lawyers in particular have heavily emphasised, a document envisaging an advanced state order\"; whereas in the case of Magna Carta there was a strong social group defending its rights against the king, \"in the Deed of Alliance there is no such party that embraced the rights contained in the deed\" — most of the ayan did not sign it, and those who did sign did not stand by it afterwards. A third view (Yaycıoğlu 2016): it was neither — it was a horizontal partnership of elites. One detail also shifts the ground of this debate: the full text of the deed was found and published only in 1998; earlier assessments relied on the incomplete text in the History of Cevdet (Târîh-i Cevdet) and in Şânîzâde (TDV `sened-i-ittifak`).", "8eb614a8"]},
"dorduncu-mustafa-sevilen-mi":{"baslik": ["Mustafa IV: The Historians' Common Verdict and the TDV Encyclopaedia's Objection", "c0acc696"], "metin": ["One question that comes to mind was this: is Mustafa IV a figure whom historians like, or has he drawn their thunderbolts upon himself? The answer is both: for a long time he was condemned almost unanimously, while in recent literature this condemnation has been openly challenged. I give both sides with their references.\n\nTHE TRADITIONAL verdict — harsh epithets. The TDV article `mustafa-iv` enumerates one by one the descriptions used by “almost all historians in general” and gives their sources: “weak in judgment and intelligence, unaware of the requirements of the age, so greedy for the throne and so merciless as to collaborate with those preparing a revolt that would endanger the survival of the state” (M. Cavid Baysun, İslâm Ansiklopedisi, VIII, 713); and “a partisan of the old order, feeble-minded, ignorant, greedy, short-sighted, a troublemaker, thoughtless, naive, unaware of the general condition of the state, a fool, one who did whatever the rebels wanted, one who thought of nothing but reigning” (Çelebizâde Âsım, Târih, II, 65; Mustafa Nûri Paşa, IV, 60; Ahmed Cevdet Paşa, Târih, VIII, 185; Mufassal Osmanlı Tarihi, V; Enver Ziya Karal, V, 84; İsmail Hami Danişmend, Kronoloji, IV, 88). The weight of this list matters: it is a chain whose links are not independent of one another and which rests largely on the same contemporary chronicles.\n\n🟢 the opposing view — the TDV article itself. Kemal Beydilli, the author of the article, says that it is “clear” that these epithets “do not accord with reality” and regards the judgment as “a matter in need of correction”. His grounds are these: ① Mustafa IV was deposed before he was given the opportunity to take any action that would show his real thinking about innovations — so no definite answer can be given to the question of whether he would have followed in his uncle's footsteps or taken a reformist path like his successor Mahmud II. ② The harsh measures he took against immorality in the city, without regard to whether the offenders were Janissaries. ③ His mentioning certain statesmen of his uncle's period, saying that if they were alive he could appoint one of them grand vizier at once, and his lamenting that no capable men were left around him. ④ His considering stationing trained soldiers in the Artillery Barracks at Taksim. ⑤ His taking a close interest, during his short reign, in the condition of the Engineering School (mühendishane), attempting to reorganize it and having a new, detailed code of regulations drawn up. ⑥ Relying on Zinkeisen (VII, 552-553): that in his reign the idea of reviving the Nizam-i Cedid was not entirely abandoned, that the sultan supported it covertly, and even that he charged Süleyman Agha, who had served as an instructor to the drilled troops at Levend Farm, with this matter.\n\n⚖️ the same article also carries the other pan of the scales. Beydilli also writes that Mustafa IV had belonged to the opposition against Selim since his years as a prince, and that those close to him, including his sister Esma Sultan, worked among the Janissaries for his accession. And there is one indisputable fact: it was he who on 28 July 1808 had the palace gates shut and ordered the killing of his cousin Selim III and his brother Mahmud; Selim was murdered, and Mahmud escaped wounded. It is also related that Mustafa attacked Mahmud in person (Mustafa Necib, Târihçe, p. 92) — this is an anecdotal report, whereas the order is not.\n\n📌 to the reader: the two sides are answering not about two different people but two different questions about the same person. On the question “What did he do?” the scale is heavily weighted, and the TDV article does not lighten it either. On the question “What would he have done?”, however, the sources are not sufficient, and the fact that one historian says openly, against all his colleagues, that “this judgment is in need of correction” shows that the second question is still open. An answer given without knowing which question you are asking does justice to neither side.", "4d70ed72"]},
"hukum-alani-mesafe":{"baslik": ["How Far Can a Capital Rule? Post Stations, Day Marches and the Campaign Season", "072f6104"], "metin": ["How far away can a capital really rule over territory? The Ottomans answered this question not in kilometers but in hours — and the records they left behind can be measured today.\n\n■ THE MENZIL: THE SPEED OF NEWS\nThe empire had set up halting points called menzil (post stations) along its roads. These were not evenly spaced; the source says so plainly: menzils were established at distances of “from three hours up to twenty-eight hours”, “according to geographical conditions, security and the places where the needs of the post station could be supplied”. In other words, from the outset the measure was not a flat distance but the difficulty of the terrain.\n\n■ HOW MANY KILOMETERS IS AN “HOUR”?\nWe have the daily register of the Uyvar campaign of 1663: each of the sixty-four halts the army passed from Davutpaşa to Buda is recorded with its hours. When measured with modern coordinates, all seven separate stages give the same band: one Ottoman hour is 4.3 to 5.4 kilometers as the crow flies. Three independent sources converge on the same figure.\n\n■ THE SAME WORD, TWO DIFFERENT SCALES\nAnd here is a distinction that escapes most readers. A KONAK is one day's march of the army: 19 kilometers on average. A MENZIL, by contrast, is the interval between the courier's stations: 85 kilometers on average. There is a four-and-a-half-fold difference between them, and the reason is simple — at the menzil the courier CHANGES HORSES and rides on; the army walks and encamps. These are two different speeds on the same road network. The place an order reaches and the place an army arrives are not the same.\n\n■ THE BOUNDARY DRAWN BY LAW\nWhat is interesting is that Islamic law, too, gave an answer to the same question. The distance required for a person to count as a “traveler” and be allowed to shorten his prayers has been estimated in all four schools of law at between 83 and 99 kilometers. In other words, in law, anything beyond this is already SOMEWHERE ELSE. This figure also agrees with the boundaries of the historical kazas (judicial districts under a qadi).\n\n■ NESTED RINGS\nWhen all this is put together, a nested order emerges: a fortress or village could truly rule up to about 40 kilometers around it, a kaza center up to 90 kilometers, a sanjak center up to 150 kilometers, and a provincial (eyalet) center up to 270 kilometers. Each tier is roughly the square root of three times the one before it — the very pattern that geographers describe in central place theory.\n\n■ AND THE REAL ANSWER: VIENNA WAS NOT TOO FAR\nThe campaign of 1663 set out from Davutpaşa on 18 March and reached Buda on 17 July: 121 days. But within those 121 days there had been only 258 hours of marching. That is, almost the whole campaign had been spent not in marching but in encamping, supplying and waiting. The army's net progress was 8.8 kilometers a day.\n\nThe campaign season — the period between the melting of the snow and its return — was roughly 240 days, and the outward march, the fighting and the return HAD TO FIT into that time. By this measure:\n\n   Going to Belgrade and back takes 184 days — comfortable.\n   Going to Buda and back takes 243 days — the whole season; no days are left for fighting.\n   Going to Vienna and back takes 290 days — it does NOT FIT into the season.\n\nThis is the answer to why the empire's northwestern frontier stopped exactly there. Vienna was not too far; it was too LATE. What drew the frontier was not a distance but a CALENDAR — and the same reckoning also places Baghdad, Algiers and Mecca among the places that could not be fitted into a single campaign. Those lands were administered by other methods, through governors, garrison corps and vassal principalities.\n\n■ THE LIMITS OF THE MEASUREMENT\nThe daily speed used here is derived from the register of a single campaign; it is not an exact figure but an ORDER OF MAGNITUDE. Even if it varied by thirty percent, Vienna would still fall outside the season — this does not change the conclusion, but one should not ascribe precision to the number.", "0770eac2"], "kisa": ["Vienna was not too far — it was too late. An empire's frontier was drawn not by distance but by the calendar.", "6d8e95c7"]},
"kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti":{"sebep.b": ["The incitement claiming that the yamaks (auxiliary garrison troops) in the Bosphorus forts were to be brought into the Nizam-i Cedid (New Order) system and made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms; the killing of Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki", "cf38e5a2"], "sonuc.b": ["Selim III's abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid with all its institutions and practices, his deposition and the accession of Mustafa IV", "4e31054c"], "bag": ["The visible face of the revolt was Kabakçı Mustafa, a Janissary from Kastamonu; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam writes that the “real architects behind the scenes” of the uprising were Köse Musa Pasha, the deputy grand vizier (kaymakam), and the Shaykh al-Islam Topal Ataullah Mehmed Efendi.", "8f1c1945"], "metin": ["The setting. The Nizam-i Cedid had been launched after the Treaty of Jassy of 1792 and had carried out important reforms, but from 1805 onward it began to be shaken by reactions and acts of protest. The Janissaries and members of the ulema who were dissatisfied with its measures found an opportunity to widen the opposition front by taking a common stand. The attempt to apply the Nizam-i Cedid in Rumelia as well came to nothing because of the resistance of the ayan (provincial notables) and armed clashes (the Second Edirne Incident, 1806) — the TDV article `selim-iii` regards this retreat as the turning point of Selim III's reign. The closure of the pilgrimage routes owing to the Wahhabi revolts and the arrival of the British fleet off Istanbul in February 1807 dealt a heavy blow to the sultan's authority; the fleet's blockade caused scarcity and high prices in the city, bringing the accumulated anger to the point of explosion.\n\nTHE SPARK. Because of the war with Russia, the army had left Istanbul on 12 April 1807. When the forts on the Bosphorus had to be reinforced against the Russian threat, the intention of bringing the yamaks there into the Nizam-i Cedid system became an opportunity for Köse Musa Pasha's covert agitation. The incitement to the effect that the yamaks would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms, and that Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki, commander of the Hungarian battery, had been assigned to this task, resulted in the killing of these two men — this is the starting point of the revolt (25 May 1807).\n\nWHY it could not be suppressed. When the uprising became known in Istanbul, Köse Musa Pasha presented it as a mere outburst of indignation; the possibility of intervention by the drilled troops at Levent Farm and the initiative of the chief of the artillery were likewise eliminated by his schemes. Selim III was being given misleading information. The figure the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam underlines is this: the sultan, who failed to show the foresight to use the Nizam-i Cedid soldiers in Istanbul, who numbered more than 10,000, first sacrificed the officials demanded of him and then agreed to the abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid. Even this did not end the revolt; he himself was deposed, and on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807 Mustafa IV was placed on the throne.\n\nTHE AFTERMATH. On 31 May 1807 a “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (a sharia court deed) was drawn up: in return for the soldiers' undertaking not to interfere in affairs of state from then on, they would not be called to account for the revolt. The deed did not hold. After the revolt Kabakçı Mustafa obtained the post of Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and fifteen days before Mahmud II was placed on the throne he was killed by Ali Agha, the ayan of Pınarhisar, sent by Alemdar Mustafa Pasha. The verdict of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is severe: with this revolt the era of renewal was closed until 1826, and the state's “great opportunity to reform itself in a way that would keep pace with the developments of the age” was thwarted.", "58445285"]},
"kimdir-dorduncu-mustafa":{"baslik": ["Who Was Mustafa IV? A Reign That Lasted Fourteen Months", "b59dbb07"], "metin": ["Birth and years as a prince. He was born on 26 Sha'ban 1193 / 8 September 1779. His father was Abdülhamid I, his mother Ayşe Sineperver Sultan. He was brought up in the palace with a classical education; together with his brother Mahmud he accompanied his father on his tours of inspection in the city. Because his cousin Selim III was childless, he received affection from him and led a comfortable life as a prince.\n\nACCESSION. When Selim III, having put an end to the Nizam-i Cedid, was deposed at the close of the Kabakçı Mustafa revolt, he became sultan on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807. He ratified the “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (sharia court deed) drawn up on 31 May 1807, stating that the rebels would not be called to account, and gave an assurance under oath. The document was of no use: those who had brought about the change of reign could not be prevented from interfering in affairs of state and forcing through whatever they wanted.\n\nTHE REIGN. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam describes these fourteen months as “such great turmoil, disorder and disintegration of the structure of the state that it would soon lead to regret over Selim's deposition”. The war with Russia that had begun in 1806 was continuing; the events in Istanbul spread to the front as well, the Janissaries revolted, the grand vizier was chased away, and the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, was killed by his own comrades. On 24 August 1807 a one-year armistice was signed at Slobozia, but this period was spent not in preparing for war but in settling internal scores. When, upon the resignation of the deputy grand vizier Köse Musa Pasha, Tayyar Mahmud Pasha, a mortal enemy of Selim III, was appointed in his place (November 1807), the Rusçuk Committee (Rusçuk yaranı), who wanted to put Selim back on the throne, gathered around Alemdar Mustafa Pasha.\n\nTHE FALL. Alemdar came to Istanbul with an army on 25 Jumada al-Ula 1223 / 19 July 1808; order was restored in the city, Kabakçı Mustafa was killed on his orders, and the Shaykh al-Islam Ataullah Mehmed Efendi was dismissed. When Alemdar stormed the Sublime Porte and headed for the palace to put Selim on the throne, Mustafa IV had the palace gates shut and ordered that his cousin Selim and his brother Mahmud be killed. Selim III was murdered; Mahmud escaped wounded and, as the last surviving member of the dynasty, was placed on the throne on 4 Jumada al-Akhira 1223 / 28 July 1808. It is related that Mustafa IV greeted the coup with the words “I have not stepped down from the throne; who has enthroned Mahmud?”, and that the sultan's imam Ahmed Kamil Efendi calmed him and saved him from Alemdar's wrath.\n\nDEATH. After being confined to his apartment in the palace, he did not refrain from sending word outside so that he might be restored to the throne. When about three months later Alemdar was attacked and killed and the rebels wished to march on the palace, he was strangled with the sash from his own waist on the order of his brother Mahmud II (28 Ramadan 1223 / 17 November 1808). His body was buried at Bahçekapı in the mausoleum of his father Abdülhamid I. Rumors spread among the rebels that the coffin was empty.\n\nFor the point on which historians differ regarding his personality, see the card `dorduncu-mustafa-sevilen-mi`.", "d41175c7"]},
"ruslara-niye-direnilemedi-1806-1812":{"baslik": ["Why could the Ottomans not hold out against the Russians in 1806-1812 — was it the army, technology, logistics or internal strife?", "25c658f9"], "ozet": ["In the summer of 1810 Sohum was lost, and in the autumn Rusçuk (Ruse). There is no one-sentence answer to the question \"Was the Russian army larger, or technically more advanced?\": the sources consulted point to at least five separate causes, and these feed one another. The card does not declare any of them to be the sole cause.", "3f5aaac8"], "metin": ["Cause ① — the throne changed hands twice while the war was going on. The war began in November 1806; in May 1807 the Kabakçı Revolt overthrew the sultan, in July 1808 a counter-coup overthrew the second sultan and brought in a third, and in November 1808 the grand vizier was killed. The TDV article `mustafa-iv` records how this was reflected at the front: \"The events in Istanbul spread here too and caused an upheaval; the Janissaries rebelled, the grand vizier was chased away, the army's senior officials fled, the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, who was handling these affairs, was killed by his own comrades, and Çelebi Mustafa Pasha was appointed grand vizier, but order could not be restored. The turmoil within the army led to a great weakness in the face of the Russians.\" Moreover, the time gained was spent not on the enemy but on internal score-settling: the one-year armistice made at Slobozia on 24 August 1807 was \"used not for taking the measures necessary to bring the war to a successful conclusion, but for the internal struggle caused by the change of sultan.\"\n\nCAUSE ② — the only modern army available was not committed to the war, and was then destroyed. Selim III had his New Order (Nizâm-ı Cedîd) army; although there were more than 10,000 trained soldiers in Istanbul in 1807, the sultan did not use them even against the revolt and abolished the corps with his own hand (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). A study examining the organisation of the campaign through the sharia court registers identifies the same gap: \"Considering that this war came on the eve of the New Order system introduced by Selim III, one would expect New Order soldiers to have taken part in it. However, Selim III did not use these soldiers in this war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). As for its successor, the new Sekban corps (Sekbân-ı Cedîd) founded in 1808, it lived for three months: on 16 November its barracks were burned, its aghas were killed and the survivors scattered (TDV `sekban-i-cedid`). The TDV article `yeniceri` states the real structural deadlock in a single sentence: between the new and old armies a \"relentless enmity\" had developed, \"so much so that even in times of need it was never possible to use these two forces together.\"\n\nCAUSE ③ — who raised the soldiers: not the centre but the provinces. The manpower as measured by the same study: \"In the research carried out on the 1806-1812 Ottoman-Russian war, it is seen that the average number of soldiers remained around 60,000\", and at the front \"alongside the soldiers of the standing army (kapıkulu), it is seen that the troops of the ayan in particular were used in the war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). The contribution of one Anatolian district (Safranbolu) is entered in the court registers in the form of demands for soldiers, avârız and imdâd-ı seferiyye taxes (extraordinary and campaign-aid levies), mules and sheep — that is, the army was assembled not through a central system of mobilisation but from soldiers and provisions gathered district by district. This is also the reason for the political weight of the ayan: the state depended on them for taxes, public order and soldiers (TDV `ayan`).\n\nCAUSE ④ — money. The revenue of the New Revenue Treasury (Îrâd-ı Cedîd Hazinesi), set up to finance the reform, had reached 64,500 purses in 1797-98; but because the growth of the new army \"increased the burden on the population of Anatolia and Rumelia, and the people were crushed under heavy taxes\", it fed the opposition, and when the New Order was abolished, \"one of the first things done was the plundering of the revenues transferred to the New Revenue Treasury\" (TDV `nizam-i-cedid`). In other words, in the middle of the war the army's source of finance was broken up.\n\nCAUSE ⑤ — several fronts and diplomacy at the same time. The war was not on a single front: the pilgrimage routes had been closed by the Wahhabi revolt, the British fleet had appeared before Istanbul in February 1807 (TDV `kabakci-isyani`), and in the Caucasus local powers had changed sides — in Abkhazia, Sefer Bey, the son of Keleş Bey and a Russian subject, wrote letters to the tsar in 1808 and 1809 stating that he wished to submit to him, and on 17 February 1810 the Russians proclaimed that Abkhazia had been joined to Russia (TDV `sohum`).\n\nAND the outcome can be read in two cities. Sohum Fortress \"fell into Russian hands on 11 July 1810 because sufficient military support could not be provided at that time\" (TDV `sohum`). Rusçuk, by contrast, \"withstood a long and bloody siege carried out by the Russian armies under the command of Count Kaminski; on 26 September 1810 it surrendered on condition that the garrison and the civilians could leave the city in safety\" (TDV `ruscuk`). Read together, the two do not yield the picture that \"Russian soldiers were better\": Rusçuk held out for months, and the following year the Russians were forced to withdraw \"after the heavy defeats and setbacks suffered in June 1811\". The issue, then, is not technical superiority in a single battle but the capacity to sustain a war: an uninterrupted supply of soldiers, money, command and political stability.\n\nWhat THIS card does not say. No quantified comparison of the Russian army with the Ottoman army of the period in terms of muskets, artillery, training and the education of officers was found in the sources consulted. The statement \"their weapons were better\" is too strong a claim to be written without being measured; the five causes above rest on measurable records, while the sixth (technical superiority) could not be measured.", "04d3cab8"], "bag": ["View: The cause was military — the old army was untrained and mutinous, and the new army was destroyed almost as soon as it was established. Counter-view: The cause was political — a state that went through two depositions, one killing of a ruler and an assassination of a grand vizier within six years could not possibly have maintained continuity at the front; the army was a result of this instability, not its cause. Third reading: both are secondary, and the real issue was financial and logistical capacity — soldiers were being gathered district by district, money was dissipated through the plundering of the reform treasury, and distant fortresses (Sohum) were falling because they could not get support. The sources present these three readings not as substitutes for one another but as intertwined.", "0b65dbbc"]},
"tabi-devlet-vassallik":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that the direct annexation of Serbia in 1439 and its conversion into a sanjak made distant lands beyond the Danube untenable in terms of the cost of defense and administration", "c33d4b54"], "sonuc.b": ["The recognition of Wallachia, in the Treaty of Edirne-Szeged, in the status of a tributary (vassal) state governed by its own voivode — the model for Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania and the Crimea over the following three centuries", "d4f4d91a"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on Wallachia (Eflak), this treaty established 'a dual sovereignty': the voivode paid tribute and remained free in internal affairs, and the land was not divided into timars — the exact opposite of the solution applied to Serbia, which in the same period was directly annexed and made into a province.", "28922bc8"], "metin": ["A tributary (vassal) state was the form of government in which the Ottomans, without annexing the territory they had conquered, left the local dynasty at its head in return for tribute and troops. Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania, the Crimean Khanate and the Sharifate of the Hejaz were different forms of this model; what they had in common was that the land was not divided into timars (military fiefs) and that local administration and religion continued. Because it secured the same control more cheaply than direct provincial rule, it was preferred especially in frontier regions that were expensive to defend.", "3eed6869"]},
"tartisma-akdeniz-vehran-onemi":{"baslik": ["Oran (Vehrân): A Port Taken and Lost for Two Centuries — Why Was It Indispensable to the Regency of Algiers?", "b7f260c9"], "metin": ["① Geography itself defended the city. Oran was founded in 290 (903) by members of the Maghrawa tribe; its name means 'two lions' or 'lions' den' in the Tuareg language. The old city was built on both banks of the Ra's al-Ayn valley, and the 375-meter Aïdour hill to its west protected the harbor from westerly winds — in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, 'its geographical structure has given the city a position that is sheltered against attacks from outside and strategic'. At a point very close to both Morocco and Spain, it was situated so as to control the sea route in the western Mediterranean. ② It was already a base in the Middle Ages. It suffered destruction repeatedly in the struggle between the Fatimids and the Andalusian Umayyads (297/910, 342/954), and passed successively into the hands of the Banu Ifran, the Zirids, the Almoravids (473/1080, Yusuf ibn Tashfin) and the Almohads; the Almohad ruler had a fleet built here and set out from here on his campaigns to take all of the Maghreb and al-Andalus. Under the Abd al-Wadids (Zayyanids) the city became the port of Tlemcen, the great trading center of the interior; for this reason the Marinids, who besieged Tlemcen in the 14th century, landed troops on the coast of Oran and had a fortress built here. When Granada fell to the Spaniards in 1492, Muslims and Jews from al-Andalus took refuge here and enlarged the city further. ③ 1509: the Spanish landing. On 17 May 1509 the Kingdom of Castile occupied Oran, killed most of its people and took the rest captive; no Muslims or Jews remained in the city. The Spaniards enlarged the walls and fortresses and turned it into a garrison town. ④ Five Ottoman attempts, four of them unsuccessful. Oruç Reis, master of Algiers, blockaded the city in 1516; but because the local rulers were uneasy at the Ottoman presence and cooperated with Spain, Oruç Reis and his brother İshak Reis were killed in 1518. Barbaros Hayreddin Pasha's attempts at recovery were not enough to retake the city either. In 1556, under Salih Pasha, beylerbey (governor-general) of Algiers, Piyale Pasha's expedition came to nothing because of the beylerbey's death. In 1563 Hasan Pasha besieged the city again but was forced to withdraw when help arrived from Spain. In the period up to 1708 the Spaniards had to contend with famine and plague as well as Berber attacks. ⑤ 1708: the first definitive Ottoman conquest — and its loss in 1732. After a long siege, the forces of Mustafa bin Yusuf Bey, who rebuilt Mascara, the center of the Western Beylik of Algiers, and who was popularly known as 'Bouchelaghem' (the big-mustached), received the surrender of Oran in 1708 in the name of the Ottomans; the news aroused great joy in Istanbul, and the bey moved his seat to the city. During the twenty-four years of Ottoman rule maritime trade revived. But in 1732 the city was retaken by Spain; with Spanish rule trade died down again and the population declined. ⑥ The earthquake of 1790 and 12 September 1792: the definitive conquest. When the great earthquake of 1790 devastated the town, the people founded a new settlement on the eastern side of the valley. On 12 September 1792 the Ottoman governor in Mascara, Kuloğlu Mehmed bin Osman, succeeded in finally retaking Oran from the Spaniards; this second conquest too was greeted with great satisfaction in Istanbul, and the people gave Mehmed Bey the title 'Mehmed (Muhammad) el-Kebir' (the Great). In total Spain held the city for 199 (1509-1708) + 60 (1732-1792) = 259 years; the 24-year Ottoman period in between falls outside this continuity. ⑦ Administrative and political importance: it became the capital of a beylik. Mehmed Bey made Oran the new center of the Western Beylik (one of the three sanjaks of the Regency of Algiers, the others being the Eastern/Constantine and Titteri/Médéa) — the TDV article on Algeria (Cezayir) records this as 'the West (center Mascara, Oran after 1792)'; moving the seat of the beylik from Mascara to Oran made the city no longer merely a port but an administrative capital. Mehmed Bey, who had the city rebuilt, settled the Muslim and Jewish population of the surrounding area here, restored to their original use the mosques that had been converted into churches, and built the mosques of Mustafa Pasha and of Mehmed Bey (Jami al-Basha), as well as a palace. ⑧ Economic importance: the gateway to Tlemcen, then the center of European trade. In the Ottoman period the city returned to its position as 'the port of Tlemcen', and maritime trade revived. After the French occupation (4 January 1831) it changed identity: while the Muslim population migrated to the interior, tens of thousands of French and other Europeans settled in the city, and Oran became the most important center of trade with Europe in Algeria. Today, too, its port competes with that of the capital, Algiers; according to 2009 data the population of the city is 852,000, and that of the province of the same name is 1,453,158.", "29bbef91"], "kisa": ["The city changed hands three times from 1509 to 1792 — behind Spain's persistence, amounting to 259 years in total, and the five separate campaigns the Ottomans undertook to win it back lay the same reason: the western gateway of the Maghreb.", "2f34ebac"], "bag": ["Its importance: because of its position, Oran was a gateway that was both easy to defend and valuable to seize — a port close to Morocco and Spain at the same time, controlling the sea route of the western Mediterranean and the trade of Tlemcen. That is why Spain held it at great expense for two and a half centuries, and the Ottomans tried to retake it in five separate campaigns. The definitive conquest of 1792 raised the city from a mere port to the capital of a beylik; only then was the coastline of the Regency of Algiers completed from end to end — and thirty-eight years later it became one of the first landing points of the French.", "aa3d7b1c"]},
"yeniceri-ocagi-kurulusu":{"sebep.b": ["The need of the principality's army, from Orhan Gazi onward, with the crossing into Rumelia and the growing conquests, for a standing, salaried infantry force tied to the center", "cfd3ee6b"], "sonuc.b": ["With the accession of Murad I, the establishment of the pençik system and of the Novice Corps (Acemi Ocağı) at Gallipoli — the nucleus of the Janissary organization", "88edaafc"], "bag": ["According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the idea of taking one-fifth (pençik) of the prisoners of war, training them and adding them to the standing army matured gradually between the end of Orhan's reign and the time of Murad I; historians still debate the exact moment of foundation, and some point to the reign of Bayezid I the Thunderbolt.", "6ddd1be6"], "metin": ["The Janissaries were the infantry branch of the kapıkulu (the sultan's salaried household troops): youths recruited through the devshirme and pençik systems were trained in the Novice Corps and then enrolled in companies called 'orta'. The number of ortas reached 165 in the reign of Suleiman the Lawgiver and later 196; the corps was organized with the ranks of çorbacı and agha and was an institution receiving a regular salary (ulufe) from the center. Mahmud II abolished the corps by turning cannon on it in the Auspicious Incident (Vak'a-i Hayriyye) of 15-16 June 1826.", "b53f43ad"]},
"yenilesme-karsiti-ayaklanmalar-etiket":{"baslik": ["“Uprisings of Those Who Did Not Want Innovation” — What the Sources Say in Five Cases", "ff766e99"], "metin": ["In Ottoman history the uprisings that broke out right after a move toward modernization form a series, and they are often told with a single formula: a rising by those opposed to innovation. Below, five cases are listed with their reasons as far as the source states them; let the reader weigh for himself or herself how far the formula holds in each case. (The scope of this card is internal to the Ottoman state; for a world comparison see the card `kiyas-yenilige-tepki-ayaklanmalari`, and for a typology of the depositions of sultans the card `tartisma-osmanli-darbeleri-tipoloji` — neither has been repeated here.)\n\n① 1622 · The Incident of Young Osman (Osman II). The revolt began on 8 Rajab 1031 / 19 May 1622, and the sultan was strangled at Yedikule the next day. The trigger was the preparation the sultan was making to go on pilgrimage: a rumor spread that this preparation was a plan to gather troops from Anatolia and do away with the Janissaries. Here the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam enters an important caveat: the reports that the sultan had given up on Istanbul, that he would march against the Janissaries with troops from Damascus and Egypt and that he would move the capital are “the propaganda of those opposed to him, which is reflected even in the sources of the period”; the account of Hüseyin Tuği, who narrated the event through the eyes of the kapıkulu troops hostile to Osman II, passed into later chronicles and led modern historiography to regard him as a ruler who was “a reformer, an innovator, wishing to carry out wide-ranging reforms”. In other words, both the portrait of the “innovative sultan” and that of a “revolt against innovation” may have been fed by the same hostile source. And the reason for the ulema's resentment was not reform but the cutting off of their arpalık (stipendiary revenue assignments).\n\n② 1730 · The Patrona Halil revolt. It began in Beyazıt on 12 Rabi al-Awwal 1143 / 25 September 1730; on the night of 1 October Damad Ibrahim Pasha was surrendered for execution, and on 2 October Ahmed III gave up the throne. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's list of causes is long, and innovation is not at the head of it: a political, economic and social reaction going back to Passarowitz, the loss of Belgrade and Temeşvar, the failure suffered in Iran, the burden of the imdadiyye (emergency aid) taxes, the granting of mukataas (revenue farms) for life, the tax burden on the artisans' guilds, and the luxurious spending of the statesmen, verging on extravagance. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam states explicitly that activities such as the opening of the printing press, the translation committee, the libraries, tile-making, the paper and textile workshops and the fire brigade “did not present a situation of concern to the broad masses of the people”; what attracted attention was the pavilions in the French architectural style, the luxury and the dissipation. Moreover, the preparation of the revolt had been undertaken by the grand vizier's own son-in-law, the kaymakam Mustafa Pasha, who had his eye on office.\n\n③ 1807 · The Kabakçı revolt. This is the case in the series that best fits the formula of “opposition to modernization”: the spark is directly the Nizam-i Cedid — the bringing of the yamaks into this system and the incitement that they would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). Yet even here the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam stand behind the scene of the revolt; for details see the card `kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti`.\n\n④ 1859 · The Kuleli Incident. A conspiratorial society that was broken up on 14 September 1859, through an informer, before it had even gone into action. The causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam are: the discontent that the Tanzimat measures produced in some quarters, the financial shock after the Crimean War, the reaction against European-style manners among the upper class, and unease over the rights granted to non-Muslims by the Reform Edict of 1856 — but at the same time soldiers and officials who “had not been brought to the positions and offices they wanted”. Here too the label is not single: Namık Kemal interpreted the Kuleli Incident as a movement for liberty and found that the secret trial of the accused by the council of ministers was contrary to the legal principles of the Rescript of Gülhane. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam also writes that the movement later influenced the Young Ottomans and the Young Turks.\n\n⑤ 1909 · The Thirty-First of March Incident. 31 March 1325 in the Rumi calendar / 13 April 1909; it lasted eleven days, the Action Army took control of the city on 24 April, and on 27 April Abdülhamid II was deposed. The soldiers of the 4th Rifle (Avcı) Battalion who mutinied rose with a demand for the sharia; among the demands the rebels conveyed to parliament was also the full application of the provisions of the sharia. But the list of causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is far broader: excessive politicization in the army, the conflict between officers risen from the ranks and academy-trained officers and the purge of the former, the loss of privileges by the old cadres, a sweeping reorganization and reduction of the bureaucracy, the struggle for power between the Committee of Union and Progress and the Ahrar (Liberal) Party, the repressive attitude of the Unionists, and assassinations by unknown perpetrators. And the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam makes the label itself a matter of debate: it was the Unionists who applied the description “reactionary uprising”; according to the article, in both Unionist and Islamist literature this description “has been seen as an instrument of the desire to bring back the era of despotism”, and “in our recent history the reactionary aspect of the 31 March Incident has been brought to the fore as more important than the incident itself”, serving as historical evidence for later politics. Moreover, Islamist figures of the period such as Elmalılı Muhammed Hamdi, Manastırlı İsmail Hakkı and Mehmed Akif openly declared that what had happened was unacceptable from the standpoint of Islam and that constitutional government could not be given up.\n\n📌 three patterns that emerge from the five cases — not a verdict, but an observation:\n· None has a single cause. In every case grievances over pay, arpalık, office and taxes are found within the same crowd as the complaint about innovation; it is often impossible to separate the two.\n· The crowd's reasons and the plotters' reasons may not be the same: in 1730 the grand vizier's son-in-law, in 1807 the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam, and in 1909 party struggle stand behind the scene.\n· It is most often the victor who has applied the label. The descriptions “reaction” in 1909 and “revolt against a reforming sultan” in 1622 come from sources that were themselves parties to the conflict. This does not mean that the uprisings were not opposed to innovation; it means that the description and the reasons must be read separately.", "5d0d6e5c"]},
"zimmi-cizye-millet-duzeni":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that with the conquest of Istanbul the empire's most populous non-Muslim communities became subjects in a single center, and the need for an institutional framework to administer this population", "e3883d25"], "sonuc.b": ["The abolition of the jizya by the Reform Edict of 1856 (Islahat Fermanı) and the imposition of the obligation of military service on non-Muslims — the legal end of the four-century-old dhimma order", "117c6dd5"], "bag": ["The TDV article on the dhimmi (Zimmî) states that the classical order was “to a significant extent abolished after the Tanzimat”; the article on the jizya (Cizye) records that the Reform Edict of 1856 abolished the jizya and that it was replaced by the military exemption tax (bedel-i askerî), which lasted until 1907. According to the article on the millet, the Armenian millet had been established in 1461 when Mehmed the Conqueror appointed a patriarch in Istanbul; after 1856 lay elements entered communal administration and the powers of the patriarchates were reduced.", "9ca5302e"], "metin": ["A dhimmi is a non-Muslim who lives as a citizen in the abode of Islam and has been given a guarantee of protection: his life and property are inviolable, his worship is free, and he has the right to take disputes within his own community to his own court; in return he recognizes the sovereignty of the state and pays the jizya. The jizya was levied only on free, adult males who were able to work and able to pay — women, children, the elderly, the blind, the disabled, the poor and clergy who had withdrawn to a life of worship were exempt — and in the Ottoman state it was divided into three classes: âlâ, the highest (48 dirhams), evsat, the middle (24), and ednâ, the lowest (12). The communities were governed through the millet organization; this organization was based not on ethnic or linguistic affiliation but on religion and denomination, which is why Gregorian, Catholic and Protestant Armenians were counted as three separate millets. The patriarch and the chief rabbi collected taxes on behalf of the state, governed the community, applied the law and supervised education; but this authority was under the control of the state — in the 17th century alone the Greek Orthodox patriarch was changed fifty-eight times.", "4b065364"]}
});
